#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-handoff — the overlapping handoff: a successor starts ALONGSIDE its predecessor, reads the journal, asks
what the journal missed, and only then retires it. A session runs it itself when its context is worth the
spend of two sessions; past the ROTATE LINE the box runs it for the session (--sweep, ROTATION below).

  cc-handoff --overlap <target> [--session ID] [--in-flight "…"]   start a successor alongside
  cc-handoff --replace <workspace> [--session ID]   a MEMBER workspace's live session, handed off onto the
                                             boundary the box has now: the overlap, then the host's own
                                             cutover once the successor's prompt lands (it cannot run
                                             --ready inside its sandbox); the old session drains and retires
  cc-handoff --ready [<target>] [--forced]   run BY THE SUCCESSOR: the one atomic cutover, then retire
  cc-handoff --abandon <target> [--why "…"]  give up: the successor's window goes, the record clears
  cc-handoff --sweep [--rotate]              from the cc-reconcile timer: finish what no session is left to;
                                             with --rotate (the tick's --apply, never a bare report) also open
                                             the overlap for a seat past the rotate line (ROTATION) or
                                             one whose skills changed (SKILL CHANGES)
  cc-handoff --kick <target> [<id>]          internal: brief the successor once it is up, check it landed
  cc-handoff --retire <target>               internal: wait out the predecessor's subagents, DRAIN it, end it
  cc-handoff --brief <target>                ONE packet of what a successor would otherwise re-read a tool
                                             call at a time — the board in it is unfinished rows, without
                                             their truncated `task:` line, and a queued row's header (and
                                             its hold, if a person put one on it) with nothing else under it;
                                             `still out` names the predecessor's running subagents and the
                                             workers it dispatched, or says it could not read its task files
                                             (--ready prints the same);
                                             a packet at or over CC_BRIEF_PACKET_BAND (24 kB) is one failures
                                             record (spend/handoff-packet-over-band), once per handoff
  cc-handoff --status [<target>] [--json]    what phase, who is live, how long left
  cc-handoff selfcheck

TARGET IS THE SESSION'S OWN TMUX WINDOW NAME in session `main`, the address the Slack layer routes on:
  `<repo>`            a session a PERSON steers in the primary worktree — planning, plain, member workspace
  `<repo>@<alias>`    an ORCHESTRATOR, and a sub-orch alongside it: same worktree, its own session
  `<repo>/<track>`    a TRACK, interactive or headless, in its own worktree
`parts` splits an address and `kind` names it. The successor's window is `<target>~next` until cutover.
A headless run (CC_ROLE=worker) is never handed off: it journals, ends its turn, and the next iteration
reads the journal on a fresh context (`worker`). A target's window is read for a loop by its processes
(held_in): a cc-loop, or a `claude -p`, held by the pane — never the text of an interactive session's own
prompt or the tool calls under it, so a successor-born seat hands itself off like any other.

THE SUCCESSOR'S BRIEF SAYS WHAT IS LIVE NOW. Its "unfinished right now" line is read off the live worker table
--brief prints, and is absent when that table is empty (flight_line); a `--in-flight` text is the caller's own
note and is labelled as such. A predecessor stays only for subagents whose transcript has not delivered its
final turn (reported()): a report already in is not waited for, however recent. Its "still out" section and
--ready's output name the predecessor's running subagents and the workers it dispatched (dispatched(): live
loops whose cc-loop `orch` file names this seat), by the same words (out_line).

THE INVARIANT. One record, ~/.cc/state/handoff/<target>.json, names the LIVE session. The live one owns the
channel and may act on the world; the other is muted and gated (cc-guard and cc-slack read the same record).
Cutover is ONE os.replace() of that record; everything after it is derived, idempotent and retried, never
rolled back. Ownership only moves forward on an explicit successful act, so a missing, corrupt or unreadable
record means the predecessor keeps the channel and the successor stays gated. A message is never handed to the
muted side: cc-slack holds it for the live one, and after the cutover and again after the retirement this file
tells cc-slackd (`{"cutover": target}`, tell_slack), which hands the successor what it held and what the
predecessor was given and never answered.

BOUNDED. deadline = started + CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP_MAX (default 20 min). The sweep IS the deadline: --sweep, on
the cc-reconcile timer, expires an overlap past it, or one whose successor's session died (two sweeps apart),
and types a `[cc-handoff]` line into the predecessor saying so (it has written its hand-off entry and is waiting
on the successor; the owner is not told), and finalizes a record whose predecessor is gone past the grace, so the survivor is not
gated by the record of its own success.

ROTATION. A seat past CC_CONTEXT_ROTATE_TOKENS (cc-context's second line, 200k, above the 150k hand-off
line the Stop hook nags at) is handed off by the box whether or not it acts on the nag: five course seats sat
on five nags each, grew to 242k-487k, and one 30-minute wake across them re-read 13M cached tokens
(2026-09-19). Every `--sweep --rotate` (cc-reconcile --apply's tick; a bare cc-reconcile reports and opens
nothing), each live window in `main` named like a target — no record open, no `~next` beside it, not a
worker's — is measured by the seat in it (seat_of: the `claude` under the pane, its `--session-id`/`--resume`;
a planning seat or an orch carries none and is measured by its checkout only when no other interactive claude
in any window of `main` shares that checkout — the peer count is taken before those filters, so a peer with
its own overlap open or misread as a worker still counts, and the checkout is read only from that seat's OWN
start, `cc-context --since`, or the session the owner quit is measured as the one he just started) through
`cc-context --json`, and `rotate` true
opens the ordinary overlap: the successor reads the journal, asks the seat its one batch, and cuts over
itself — the seat's own transcript and journal stay the packet, and no window is killed under it.
`<slug>.rotated` (~/.cc/state/handoff), written only when the overlap opened, holds the box off a target for
CC_HANDOFF_ROTATE_RETRY (1 h), so an overlap that expired is not reopened every tick; a start that refused
is tried again next tick.
A seat the sweep cannot find under its pane or cannot measure (a member workspace's, inside its sandbox, whose
transcript is not under ~/.claude/projects) is left alone.

SKILL CHANGES. A session reads its skills only at start, so the same `--sweep --rotate` catches a seat whose
skill set changed — cc-skills sync, a hand sync and a landing that edits a skill all end as a changed file, so the
sweep reads the files and none of them has to tell it. A seat's skills are the host's (~/.claude/skills; a member
seat only the names `cc-skills names` binds into its sandbox) and its checkout's .claude/skills. Its set CHANGED
when a SKILL.md there arrived or changed after the seat started or last reloaded (skill_changes; a skill removed is
not noticed). The seat is then typed `/reload-skills` (Claude Code's own "pick up skills added or changed on disk
during this session", in every version installed here since 2.1.277), which costs one short turn; only a seat whose
claude binary has no such command (can_reload) gets the ordinary overlap instead. BOUNDED: a seat is taken only when
it has had a turn since the change (its transcript, else its pane, is newer than the change), so a dormant seat waits
until someone uses it; only while it is idle, never mid-turn; at most CC_HANDOFF_SKILL_MAX (2; 0 turns it off) a
tick, the rest on later ticks; a context rotation is never doubled by it. `<slug>.skills` records what was done
and when: a reload moves the seat's "since" to then, and a handoff holds the box off that target for
CC_HANDOFF_ROTATE_RETRY, as `.rotated` does.

QUIET WHILE OPEN. start() marks the target `~/.cc/state/<target>/pulse.off` (cc-reconcile's wake honours it)
and takes the mark back at cutover and in clear(). A mark this file did not make is never removed.

RETIREMENT DRAINS THE PREDECESSOR, THEN ENDS IT. At cutover the names move: the predecessor becomes
`<target>~old` and the successor takes `<target>`. The predecessor keeps its window for CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN, or
until it is idle with nothing typed, never less than CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE; then the window is killed. A
predecessor with live subagents STAYS first (read off the harness's task files; silence for
CC_HANDOFF_AGENT_IDLE counts as done; capped at CC_HANDOFF_AGENT_MAX), hands each report to the successor, and
gets the final message when the last one is in. Its board rows held by an `agent` mark move to the successor
with the name (carry_marks). The kill is fenced by refuse_kill(): only the window the record names, only after
cutover, never the caller's own. A seat whose record carries no session id (a planning seat, an orch) has it
read off its live pane at --overlap and again at --ready (seat_sid). Task files are read under both /tmp and
TMPDIR (task_roots); a predecessor whose task files are found under neither is named as unread in `still out`, never
as having none.
A bounded stay, not a refusal and not a wait before cutover: the successor is live at once, and the staying
predecessor spends nothing between reports. A worker the seat dispatched runs in a window of its own
(its dispatch opens one), which nothing here kills, so it outlives the handoff and the retirement; its stop
notice is addressed to the target (cc-loop's stop_target), never to a window.

EVERY PROMPT THIS FILE TYPES INTO A SESSION LEADS WITH `[cc-handoff]` (MACHINE): cc-scope's ask ledger skips it
by the tag, never by the wording.

Env: CC_HANDOFF (the successor's own record id, exported by `cc __runnext`; it outlives the handoff, so
`<id>.done` and not the variable says one is still open) · CC_HANDOFF_WINDOW (the successor's own tmux
window, so it accepts its own startup dialog) · CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP_MAX, CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE,
CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN, CC_HANDOFF_DONE_TTL, CC_HANDOFF_KICK_WAIT, CC_HANDOFF_GONE_CONFIRM, CC_HANDOFF_AGENT_IDLE,
CC_HANDOFF_AGENT_MAX, CC_HANDOFF_ROTATE_RETRY, CC_HANDOFF_SKILL_MAX (~/.cc/config) · CC_HANDOFF_DIR, CC_CTX_RECORDS, CC_HANDOFF_TASKS,
CC_HANDOFF_SKILLS (test hooks) ·
CC_BRIEF_WORKER_IDLE (--brief: how quiet a track's loop.log may be and still count as live, 1 h) ·
CC_BRIEF_PACKET_BAND (--brief: the packet size, in bytes, that is recorded as context cost, 24 kB).
"""
import fcntl, glob, json, os, re, shlex, shutil, socket, subprocess, sys, tempfile, time, uuid

STATE = os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/state")
DEV = os.path.expanduser("~/dev")        # where the checkouts are: --brief asks gh inside the target's own
HANDOFF = os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF_DIR") or os.path.join(STATE, "handoff")
RECORDS = os.environ.get("CC_CTX_RECORDS") or os.path.join(STATE, "context")
SKILLS = os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF_SKILLS") or os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/skills")   # the host's skills (SKILL CHANGES)
BIN = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
SESSION = "main"
NEXT = "~next"                     # the successor's window suffix; `~` is not legal in a repo/track name
OLD = "~old"                       # ...and the predecessor's, from cutover to the end of its drain
DEFAULT_MAX = 20 * 60              # the overlap's cap — see BOUNDED, AND SHORT; CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP_MAX overrides it
QUIET_MARK = "pulse.off"           # the file cc-reconcile's wake reads to leave a target alone, and the only mark we write
NAME = r"[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}"
ALIAS = r"[a-z0-9-]{2,20}"         # `cc <repo> --orch <alias>` accepts exactly this, so this accepts exactly it
TARGET_RE = re.compile(rf"^{NAME}(@{ALIAS}|/{NAME})?$")   # the three shapes above, and nothing else
MACHINE = "[cc-handoff]"           # every prompt this file types into a session leads with it — see the header


def tell_slack(target):
    """Say the cutover to cc-slackd, `{"cutover": target}`, so what it held while no live side was subscribed, and
    what the predecessor was handed and never answered, reach the successor NOW rather than on its 30 s tick (the
    brief, 2026-09-24: "queued and delivered to the successor at --ready"). Best effort: the tick is the fallback,
    so a daemon that is down or slow costs seconds, never the message. A test's own CC_HANDOFF_DIR with no
    CC_SLACK_DIR of its own says nothing to the box's real daemon."""
    if os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF_DIR") and not os.environ.get("CC_SLACK_DIR"):
        return
    sock = os.path.join(os.environ.get("CC_SLACK_DIR") or os.path.expanduser("~/.cc/slack"), "sock")
    try:
        with socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM) as s:
            s.settimeout(5)
            s.connect(sock)
            s.sendall((json.dumps({"cutover": target}) + "\n").encode())
            s.recv(256)
    except OSError:
        pass


def cfg(name, default):
    """A number from the environment, else ~/.cc/config — through cc-config, which applies that precedence
    for every tool on the box and never sources the file (it holds tokens)."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-config"), "get", name], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10)
        v = r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None
    except Exception:
        v = None
    try:
        return float(v)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return default


# ---- the address -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE PLACE SPLITS AN ADDRESS. Every phase below used to do `target.partition("/")` and call the left half the
# repo, which is true of two shapes out of three: for `<repo>@<alias>` it made the repo the whole `repo@alias`
# string, and the brief packet, the ledger row and the journal path all went looking for a repo of that name.

def parts(target):
    """(repo, alias, track) for an address. Permissive on purpose — TARGET_RE is the gate, and a parser that
    also survives a shape the gate rejects is one that cannot mangle it into a wrong repo name."""
    head, _, track = (target or "").partition("/")
    repo, _, alias = head.partition("@")
    return repo, alias, track


def kind(target):
    """Which kind of session this address names. `session` is one a PERSON steers — planning, plain, a member
    workspace: the primary worktree, no alias. `orch` is an orchestrator or a sub-orch beside it, same worktree
    and a name of its own. `track` is a track in its own worktree, interactive or headless. A track under an
    orch is still a track: it has its own worktree and its own window, and the two must never be confused."""
    repo, alias, track = parts(target)
    if track:
        return "track"
    return "orch" if alias else "session"


ARTICLE = {"session": "a session a person steers", "orch": "an orchestrator", "track": "a track"}
SHAPES = "<repo>, <repo>@<alias> or <repo>/<track>"
WORKER_REFUSAL = ("a worker iteration is not handed off — it journals, ends its turn, and its loop starts the "
                  "next one with a fresh context")   # one text: worker() and worker_held() are the same refusal


# ---- the record ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def slug(target):
    """One flat filename per address. `@` needs no escaping and cannot collide with the `--` a track uses, so
    `r1@a`, `r1/a` and `r1--a` stay three different records."""
    return target.replace("/", "--")


def path(target):
    return os.path.join(HANDOFF, slug(target) + ".json")


def read(target):
    """The open overlap for this target, or None. Missing and corrupt read the same on purpose: both mean
    'nobody has taken over', which is the direction every failure here must fall."""
    try:
        with open(path(target)) as f:
            r = json.load(f)
    except Exception:
        return None
    return r if isinstance(r, dict) and r.get("id") and r.get("target") else None


def read_all():
    out = []
    for n in sorted(os.listdir(HANDOFF) if os.path.isdir(HANDOFF) else []):
        if not n.endswith(".json"):
            continue
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(HANDOFF, n)) as f:
                r = json.load(f)
        except Exception:
            continue                      # one bad record never hides the rest
        if isinstance(r, dict) and r.get("id") and r.get("target"):
            out.append(r)
    return out


def by_id(hid):
    for r in read_all():
        if r.get("id") == hid:
            return r
    return None


def write(rec):
    """THE COMMIT POINT. One os.replace(): every consumer sees either the whole old record or the whole new
    one, and there is no instant in which nobody is in charge."""
    os.makedirs(HANDOFF, exist_ok=True)
    p = path(rec["target"])
    tmp = p + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(rec, f)
    os.replace(tmp, p)
    return rec


def brief_path(target):
    return os.path.join(HANDOFF, slug(target) + ".brief")


def quiet_path(target):
    """The drive loop's own opt-out for this address — `~/.cc/state/<target>/pulse.off`, keyed exactly as
    cc-pulse keys it, so `r1` and `r1@dash` are separate files and a track's sits under its repo."""
    return os.path.join(STATE, target, QUIET_MARK)


def quiet_claim(target):
    """Which marks this overlap may make — [] when a pulse.off is already there, because that one is somebody
    else's decision and is neither rewritten nor, later, taken away. Asked BEFORE the record is written, so the
    record can name the mark before the mark exists: a crash between the two leaves nothing to remove, while a
    mark no record names would leave the target out of the drive loop for good."""
    return [] if os.path.exists(quiet_path(target)) else [QUIET_MARK]


def quiet(target, marks):
    """Make the marks the record has already claimed. Mark the retiring session quiet for the overlap: the
    successor's questions are the only thing worth a full-context wake from a session that is handing over."""
    if QUIET_MARK not in (marks or []):
        return []
    p = quiet_path(target)
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p), exist_ok=True)
        with open(p, "w") as f:
            f.write("cc-handoff: an overlap is open on %s — the drive loop leaves the retiring session alone "
                    "until it is over. Removed by cc-handoff itself.\n" % target)
    except OSError:
        return []                                  # a mark we could not write is one nobody waits on
    return [QUIET_MARK]


def unquiet(rec):
    """Take back the marks THIS overlap made, and nothing else. Called at cutover — from that instant the name
    belongs to the successor and the drive loop must reach it — and again from clear(), which every path that
    ends an overlap goes through, so a crashed cutover still leaves no target muted for good."""
    if not rec:
        return []
    gone = []
    for m in rec.get("quiet") or []:
        if m != QUIET_MARK:                        # the record is a file: never build a path out of what it says
            continue
        try:
            os.remove(quiet_path(rec["target"]))
            gone.append(m)
        except OSError:
            pass
    if gone and os.path.exists(path(rec["target"])):   # only ever stamp a record still on disk
        rec["quiet"] = [m for m in (rec.get("quiet") or []) if m not in gone]
        write(rec)
    return gone


def clear(target):
    unquiet(read(target))          # the record is still on disk here, and it is what says which marks are ours
    for f in (path(target), brief_path(target)):
        try:
            os.remove(f)
        except OSError:
            pass


ID_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$")       # a record id, and safe to build a filename from


def done_path(hid):
    """Where a COMPLETED retirement is remembered, keyed on the successor's own $CC_HANDOFF id."""
    return os.path.join(HANDOFF, hid + ".done") if ID_RE.match(hid or "") else None


def mark_done(rec):
    """Evidence that the retirement FINISHED, and it must outlive clear(). The successor keeps $CC_HANDOFF for
    the life of its process (by design — the model must not be able to unset it), so without this a handoff
    that went RIGHT is indistinguishable from a record lost because something went WRONG, and cc-guard gates
    the only session left. On 2026-08-31 that bricked the session that won the first real handoff: it lost
    dispatch mid-sentence. Written BEFORE clear(), so there is no instant in which the record is gone and the
    reason it is gone is not on disk."""
    p = done_path(rec.get("id") or "")
    if not p:
        return False
    os.makedirs(HANDOFF, exist_ok=True)
    tmp = p + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump({"id": rec["id"], "target": rec.get("target"), "retired": time.time()}, f)
    os.replace(tmp, p)
    ttl = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_DONE_TTL", 30 * 86400)   # once, not once per marker
    for n in sorted(os.listdir(HANDOFF)):          # no session outlives the TTL, so an older marker is litter
        if n.endswith(".done") and n != os.path.basename(p):
            q = os.path.join(HANDOFF, n)
            try:
                if os.path.getmtime(q) < time.time() - ttl:
                    os.remove(q)
            except OSError:
                pass
    return True


def worker():
    """Is this turn a HEADLESS cc-loop iteration? Then it takes no phase: it journals, ENDS ITS TURN, and the
    loop starts the next iteration with a fresh context reading that journal — that IS the handoff, and it
    costs no second session. CC_ROLE=worker is the tell (cc-loop exports it around its `claude -p`, cc-secretary
    around its own, and a Stop hook inherits it — cc-scope reads the same variable the same way).
    The tmux window is NOT the tell, which is the whole bug: the LOOP holds the track's window while the session
    inside it is headless, so a worker read as `live`. On 2026-09-01 spend-tracker's iteration opened an overlap
    on that reading, and the INTERACTIVE successor it started finished the work, ran `cc done`, and then sat in
    the track window forever — the Home tally showed the track RUNNING over a stale board."""
    return os.environ.get("CC_ROLE") == "worker"


def worker_held(wid):
    """Is that WINDOW a headless worker's — a cc-loop — rather than a session's? worker() asks the CALLER, and the
    caller is the hole: `cc handoff <repo> <track>` from a planning seat passes it, win_id() then finds the LOOP's
    window, and the record gates the running `claude -p` through cc-guard mid-iteration while an interactive
    successor spends beside it — the 2026-09-01 incident worker() describes, reached from the outside. So the
    target is asked too, by the only thing that knows: its own pane's processes (held_in decides; this reads them).
    UNREADABLE SAYS HELD. A pane that will not answer — tmux gone, the window dead, ps timed out — returns True,
    because the two mistakes do not cost the same: a wrong refusal costs a retry, a wrong allow costs the incident
    above, a gated worker and a second paid session in its worktree. start() has already resolved the window by
    the time it asks, so an unreadable pane there is an anomaly, not the ordinary case. A foreground lander in an
    interactive pane is the one false positive, which refuses safely."""
    if not wid:
        return False
    pids = (tmux("list-panes", "-t", wid, "-F", "#{pane_pid}") or "").split()
    if not pids:
        return True                     # win_id() resolved it a moment ago: no panes now is a misread, not an answer
    try:
        ps = subprocess.run(["ps", "-eo", "pid=,ppid=,args="], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10).stdout
    except Exception:
        return True                     # same: no answer is not "not a worker"
    return held_in(pids, ps)


# recurring-defect-ok: pause-hold-missing — this file launches no worker: `claude -p` and cc-loop here are the tokens held_in READS in a pane to refuse a handoff of a worker's window
ENV_TOKEN = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=")   # `CC_CLAUDE=/…/claude` in a `bash -c` line: a setting, not a command


def held_in(pids, ps):
    """Pure: does the process tree under the pane's `pids` (`ps -eo pid=,ppid=,args=` text) hold a headless worker?
    THE LOOP IS THE TELL, NOT THE ITERATION. `cc --go` makes the window as `cc-sandbox … -- cc-loop …; exec bash`
    (core/bin/cc), so cc-loop is alive between iterations too, and when it ends `exec bash` leaves a bare shell
    that answers False. Reading only the iteration's `claude -p` would wave a handoff through in the gap between
    two of them and gate the NEXT one. Both are read, each as a TOKEN and never as a substring: a headless prompt
    carries the whole brief in argv and matches anything you grep for.
    AN INTERACTIVE SESSION ENDS THE READ. A `claude` token with no `-p`/`--print` beside it is a session a person
    or a successor drives: the tokens after it are its system prompt (a successor's carries its handoff brief —
    which names cc-loop and `claude -p` as a matter of course), and the processes under it are its own tool
    calls (a grep for cc-loop, a Monitor tailing a loop log). Neither is a worker. Until 2026-09-19 both were
    scanned, and `cc handoff --overlap lessons` refused a successor-born planning seat as "a headless worker's
    window" at the most expensive moment of its life. A `NAME=value` token is skipped, not read as a command:
    the loop's own pane line starts `CC_CLAUDE=/…/claude …/cc-loop`."""
    kids, argv = {}, {}
    for line in ps.splitlines():
        parts = line.split(None, 2)
        if len(parts) == 3:
            kids.setdefault(parts[1], []).append(parts[0])
            argv[parts[0]] = parts[2]
    seen, stack = set(), list(pids)
    while stack:
        pid = stack.pop()
        if pid in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(pid)
        toks = argv.get(pid, "").split()
        descend = True
        for i, t in enumerate(toks):
            if ENV_TOKEN.match(t):
                continue
            base = os.path.basename(t)
            if base == "cc-loop":
                return True
            if base == "claude":
                if set(toks[i + 1:i + 4]) & {"-p", "--print"}:
                    return True
                descend = False              # an interactive session: its argv tail and its children are its own
                break
        if descend:
            stack += kids.get(pid, [])
    return False


def live_side(rec):
    return "successor" if rec.get("live") == "successor" else "predecessor"


def ledger(rec, kind, why=""):
    """One countable line per phase — the handoff count stays a `wc -l`. `phases` in the record is what makes
    it once and not once per retry; an abandoned record is deleted, so a second --abandon is a no-op."""
    if kind in rec.setdefault("phases", []):
        return False
    rec["phases"].append(kind)
    os.makedirs(RECORDS, exist_ok=True)
    repo, alias, track = parts(rec["target"])
    # ISO, never a float: cc-slack's home_handoffs() buckets these rows by local day, and after this PR
    # deletes cc-context's ledger() this file is their only writer (review of #501).
    row = {"ts": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()), "kind": kind, "handoff": rec["id"], "target": rec["target"],
           "repo": repo, "alias": alias or None, "track": track or None, "pct": rec.get("pct_at_start"),
           "session_id": (rec.get(live_side(rec)) or {}).get("session_id")}
    if why:
        row["why"] = why
    with open(os.path.join(RECORDS, "handoffs.jsonl"), "a") as f:
        f.write(json.dumps(row) + "\n")
    return True


# ---- tmux ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def tmux(*args, timeout=10):
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["tmux", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
        return r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 else None
    except Exception:
        return None


def win_id(name):
    """Address windows by id: names with `.` or `/` break `-t main:<name>`."""
    for l in (tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id} #W") or "").splitlines():
        wid, _, n = l.partition(" ")
        if n == name:
            return wid
    return None


def pane_live_id(wid):
    """A window whose wrapper shell has a child = a real session; a bare fallback shell has none."""
    if not wid:
        return False
    pids = (tmux("list-panes", "-t", wid, "-F", "#{pane_pid}") or "").split()
    if not pids:
        return False
    try:
        return subprocess.run(["pgrep", "-P", pids[0]], capture_output=True).returncode == 0
    except Exception:
        return False


def pane_live(name):
    return pane_live_id(win_id(name))


def side_live(rec, side):
    """Is that side's SESSION still there — asked BOTH ways, because either one alone says 'gone' too easily: by
    the window name it should hold, and by the window id the record names (a renamed window is still that
    session). The sweep un-gates on this answer, so a false 'gone' is the expensive direction."""
    s = rec.get(side) or {}
    return pane_live(s.get("window") or "") or pane_live_id(s.get("tmux") or "")


def win_alive(wid):
    """Is that window id still on the server? Ids are never reused, so this is the one safe way to ask."""
    return bool(wid) and wid in (tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id}") or "").split()


def side_wid(rec, side):
    """That side's window: by the name it should hold, else by the id the record names if it is still there.
    Both, because an expiry that finds neither leaves a successor nobody will ever finish (2026-09-01: six)."""
    s = rec.get(side) or {}
    return win_id(s.get("window") or "") or (s.get("tmux") if win_alive(s.get("tmux") or "") else None)


def age(pid):
    """Seconds since that pane's shell started — the window's age; tmux keeps no creation time."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["ps", "-o", "etimes=", "-p", str(pid)], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
        return int(r.stdout.strip() or 0)
    except Exception:
        return 0


def orphans():
    """Windows named like a side of a handoff — `<target>~next`, `<target>~old` — whose target has NO record:
    nothing driven from a record will ever finish them. [(name, window id, age in s)], oldest first."""
    out = []
    for l in (tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id} #{pane_pid} #W") or "").splitlines():
        col = l.split(" ", 2)                      # not `parts` — that is the address splitter, module-level
        if len(col) < 3:
            continue
        wid, pid, name = col
        for suf in (NEXT, OLD):
            if name.endswith(suf) and TARGET_RE.match(name[:-len(suf)]) and not read(name[:-len(suf)]):
                out.append((name, wid, age(pid)))
    return sorted(out, key=lambda x: -x[2])


def unaddressable():
    """Live windows in session `main` running a SESSION whose name this file cannot take as an address.
    Every kind the box starts wears one of the three shapes in the header, so this list is normally empty —
    it is a tripwire, not a queue. A kind that stops being recognised, by a new launcher or by a rename, is a
    session that can never be handed off and can never be told apart from its neighbours in the measurement;
    until 2026-09-07 that was quietly true of every orchestrator on every box. [(name, window id, age in s)]."""
    out = []
    for l in (tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id} #{pane_pid} #W") or "").splitlines():
        col = l.split(" ", 2)
        if len(col) < 3:
            continue
        wid, pid, name = col
        if name.endswith(NEXT) or name.endswith(OLD):
            continue                               # a side of a handoff in flight: `orphans` owns those
        if TARGET_RE.match(name) or not pane_live_id(wid):
            continue                               # …and a window with no session in it is not a session
        out.append((name, wid, age(pid)))
    return out


def self_wid():
    """The window THIS process is running in, from $TMUX_PANE — never a bare `display-message`, which answers
    with whatever window is ACTIVE and would cheerfully name somebody else's session."""
    pane = os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE") or ""
    if not pane:
        return ""
    return (tmux("display-message", "-p", "-t", pane, "#{window_id}") or "").strip()


def handoff_log():
    """Where a DETACHED child's one line goes. Resolved at CALL time, from $CC_CTX_RECORDS then $HOME — the
    two things a test redirects. It followed neither (it rebuilt the path from `~` at the moment of the call,
    and RECORDS was fixed at import), so a selftest's own `--retire` wrote _cctest lines into the box's REAL
    ~/.cc/state/context/handoff.log — 2026-09-01, found in it."""
    return os.path.join(os.environ.get("CC_CTX_RECORDS")
                        or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".cc", "state", "context"), "handoff.log")


def run(argv, wait=True):
    try:
        if wait:
            return subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60).returncode == 0
        log = handoff_log()
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(log), exist_ok=True)   # a detached fallback that REFUSES must leave its one line somewhere
        out = open(log, "a")
        subprocess.Popen(argv, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=out, stderr=out, start_new_session=True)
        return True
    except Exception:
        return False


def notify(title, msg):
    run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-notify"), "-t", title, msg])


def alert(target, msg):
    """#alerts (owner, 2026-09-01): every handover leaves one line there — and it lands there because the
    `alerts` kind is rung 4 in cc-notify's table, not because this file names a channel. Naming one cost
    twice: the line skipped the table altogether, and the test-state rule that lives at cc-notify's door had
    to be kept here as a second copy (the Slack layer applies none of it, and three _cctest handovers hit the
    REAL #alerts the first night this shipped). One door, one rule, and the line is in ~/.cc/notify.log.

    THE TITLE IS WHAT THE TABLE READS, and it must not start with the emoji the line carries: `kind_of` keys
    on the title's SECOND word, so "🔁 <target>: handover" made that word the target's own name — no row, so
    ambient — and `scope_of` would key every handover on one emoji. The emoji stays in the message, where it
    is for the reader. Best-effort like notify(): a handover must never fail on Slack being down."""
    run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-notify"), "--kind", "alerts", "-t", f"{target} handover", msg])


# ---- the predecessor's subagents -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def task_roots():
    """Where the harness may keep task files: <tmp>/claude-<uid>, where <tmp> is the TMPDIR of the claude process
    that runs the session — /tmp for some seats, ~/.cc/tmp for others (both probed 2026-09-24). This process's own
    TMPDIR says nothing about the predecessor's: a seat's Bash runs with TMPDIR=~/.cc/tmp, so reading that root
    alone found no task files for a predecessor that wrote under /tmp, and the chip-flow packet said its three
    running builders were (none) (2026-09-24 20:02Z; the successor re-dispatched into two of their worktrees).
    So both are read. CC_HANDOFF_TASKS replaces them (os.pathsep-separated) — for a fixture, and for a harness
    that moves them."""
    env = os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF_TASKS")
    roots = env.split(os.pathsep) if env else [os.path.join(t, "claude-%d" % os.getuid())
                                              for t in (tempfile.gettempdir(), "/tmp")]
    return list(dict.fromkeys(os.path.realpath(r) for r in roots if r))


def tasks_dirs(sid):
    """Where the harness keeps what session `sid` is running: <root>/<project>/<session id>/tasks under any of
    task_roots(), one `<id>.output` per task. The project part is that session's own directory, so it is globbed
    and never guessed."""
    return [d for r in task_roots() for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(r, "*", sid, "tasks"))] if sid else []


REPORT_TAIL = 65536   # bytes of a transcript's end read for its last record — cc-slack's HOME_AGENT_TAIL, the same read


def reported(path, tail=REPORT_TAIL):
    """Has this subagent handed its report in? THE SAME RULE AS cc-slack's home_agent_done, and the two must stay
    in step — a transcript one calls finished the other must not wait for (review of #542: the first cut here
    keyed on `stop_reason: end_turn`, and 431 of 824 finished transcripts on the box end in an assistant record
    with `stop_reason: null`, which the Home tab called done and --ready would have held the predecessor for).
    The rule: the transcript's last record is an `assistant` record whose content carries no `tool_use` block —
    a final report is text; a tool call is mid-turn. A `user` record (the parent resumed it), a record cut
    mid-write, a tail read that landed inside the last record (size over `tail` with fewer than two lines), a
    file that is unreadable or not JSON: False — the mtime rule below still decides, as it did before this existed,
    because calling a live agent finished is the costly mistake. Read from the tail, never the whole file (a
    transcript runs to hundreds of kB). Change this and cc-slack's together, or --ready and the Home tab disagree
    about the same transcript."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            f.seek(0, 2)
            size = f.tell()
            f.seek(max(0, size - tail))
            chunk = f.read()
    except OSError:
        return False
    lines = [ln for ln in chunk.split(b"\n") if ln.strip()]
    if not lines or (size > tail and len(lines) < 2):     # the cut landed inside the last record: no answer either way
        return False
    try:
        rec = json.loads(lines[-1].decode("utf-8", "replace"))
    except Exception:
        return False                           # a record caught mid-write is not a finished agent
    if not isinstance(rec, dict) or rec.get("type") != "assistant":
        return False
    c = (rec.get("message") or {}).get("content")
    return not any(isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "tool_use" for b in (c if isinstance(c, list) else []))


def subagents(sid, idle=None):
    """Session `sid`'s subagents that still look ALIVE, the busiest first. A `<id>.output` that is a SYMLINK is
    a subagent — it points at the transcript that agent is writing; a plain file beside it is a background shell
    command (a Monitor too), whose output nobody has to hand on. Two things say "finished": the transcript's own
    last record (reported(): the agent's final report, an assistant record with no tool call in it, read by the
    rule cc-slack's Home tab reads it — a report that is in is not waited for, whether it came in five minutes
    ago or five hours ago), and silence: a transcript
    untouched for CC_HANDOFF_AGENT_IDLE counts as done. The default is 30 min because an agent blocked inside a
    SINGLE Bash call — a gate run, a long build — writes nothing at all while it waits, and a live one read as
    finished is the expensive mistake here. Before reported() existed the silence rule alone held a predecessor
    open for subagents whose reports were journaled and landed (2026-09-15, and twice on 2026-09-16).
    Unknown session id, no such files, a harness that stops keeping them: [], and every caller then does what it
    did before this existed. cc-checkpoint (the Stop hook) counts no subagents at all — the "uncommitted work in
    agent-…" line a 2026-09-16 handoff was held on was the old cc-context's, gone with #501."""
    if idle is None:
        idle = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_AGENT_IDLE", 1800)
    now, live = time.time(), []
    for d in tasks_dirs(sid):
        for f in glob.glob(os.path.join(d, "*.output")):
            if not os.path.islink(f):
                continue
            try:
                m = os.stat(f).st_mtime            # THROUGH the link: the link itself is written once and never again
            except OSError:
                continue                           # dangling: a transcript that is gone is nobody's report
            if now - m < idle and not reported(f):
                live.append((m, os.path.basename(f)[:-len(".output")]))
    return [n for _, n in sorted(live, reverse=True)]


def tasks_sid(pid):
    """The session id of claude process `pid`, off the task directory it holds open: every live interactive
    claude keeps an fd on its own `<root>/<project>/<session id>/tasks` (probed 2026-09-22, ten seats, one fd
    each). "" when it holds none or cannot be read."""
    pat = re.compile("(?:" + "|".join(re.escape(r) for r in task_roots()) + r")/[^/]+/([A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*)/tasks$")
    try:
        fds = os.listdir(f"/proc/{pid}/fd")
    except OSError:
        return ""
    for fd in fds:
        try:
            m = pat.match(os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/fd/{fd}"))
        except OSError:
            continue
        if m:
            return m.group(1)
    return ""


def seat_sid(wid):
    """The session id of the seat in window `wid`, read off its live process (tasks_sid) — the one way to find the
    subagents of a seat whose record carries no id. A planning seat and an orch never do (seat_of): until
    2026-09-22 their subagents read as none, and --ready retired the voice seat two minutes after cutover with
    three of them 12-15 minutes in, all three lost (2026-09-21 19:57Z)."""
    pid = pane_claude(wid)
    return tasks_sid(pid) if pid else ""


def pre_sid(rec):
    """The predecessor's session id: the record's, else read off its live pane (seat_sid)."""
    pre = (rec or {}).get("predecessor") or {}
    return pre.get("session_id") or seat_sid(pre.get("tmux") or "")


def dispatched(target):
    """The workers this seat dispatched that are still running: live loops under its repo (live_workers) whose
    `orch` file (cc-loop's orch_record) names this orch's alias — or names none, for the repo's own seat. A track
    dispatches none. They run in windows of their own, which the retirement never kills (refuse_kill), so they
    outlive the handoff; the successor is the one they report to, and it must know they are out."""
    repo, alias, track = parts(target)
    if track:
        return []
    out = []
    for line in live_workers(repo).splitlines():
        t = line.split(None, 1)[0] if line.strip() else ""
        try:
            who = open(os.path.join(STATE, repo, t, "orch")).read().strip()
        except OSError:
            who = ""
        if t and who == (alias or ""):
            out.append(t)
    return out


def out_line(target, rec):
    """What the predecessor still has out, in one line — the same words in --brief and --ready."""
    sid = pre_sid(rec)
    subs, wk = subagents(sid), dispatched(target)
    bits = []
    if not sid or not tasks_dirs(sid):
        # "(none)" must mean none were found, never that nobody could look: a successor reads it as leave to
        # re-dispatch into the predecessor's worktrees (chip-flow, 2026-09-24)
        bits.append("its subagents could not be read (" + (f"no task files for session {sid}" if sid else
                    "no session id") + "): ask it which are still running before you dispatch into a worktree")
    if subs:
        bits.append(f"{len(subs)} subagent(s) still running ({', '.join(subs[:6])}): it stays for them after "
                    f"cutover and hands each report on to you")
    if wk:
        bits.append(f"{len(wk)} worker(s) it dispatched ({', '.join(wk[:6])}): they keep running through the "
                    f"handoff, and their stop notices are addressed to {target}, not to its window")
    return "; ".join(bits)


# ---- phases --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

BRIEF = """You are the SUCCESSOR in an overlapping handoff of {target}. THE OVERLAP IS SHORT: {mins} minutes at \
most, because every minute of it runs two sessions of this size, and it ends the moment you run \
`cc-handoff --ready`. So the order is fixed — read the packet, ask your ONE batch of questions FIRST, and go \
ready as soon as they are answered. Anything you could do just as well alone five minutes later, do it then. \
Your FIRST tool call is \
`cc-handoff --brief {target}` — one packet holding the checkpoint view of {journal} (its newest entries, 8 kB; \
`cc-lib ask --need history` reaches the older ones), \
the unfinished board, what is landing, the live workers, the open PRs and the open asks. That packet plus what \
your predecessor tells you is everything you start from; rebuilding it a call at a time is what made a handover \
cost about two million tokens. You are NOT the live session yet: the live one owns this target's channel and \
may act on the world, and cc-guard refuses merging, pushing, deploying, dispatching workers, `cc done` and \
outward posts from you until you take over. Read, search, think and ask — and NEVER ask your predecessor to run \
something you are denied. Your predecessor is still working in this same directory: it is the peer whose tmux \
id is {tmux} in `ListAgents`, so reach it with `SendMessage` (bidirectional, out of band from the channel) and \
ask it ONE batch of questions — everything the packet did not answer, in a single message, because it answers \
in one turn and each turn of its costs a whole context; subscribe with notify_when_idle, which is also how you \
hear if it exits. {flight} When you have the context and NOT BEFORE, run `cc-handoff --ready` — that single \
call is the commit point: it makes you live and retires your predecessor. If you cannot get there, say so in \
the journal; the overlap expires on its own and your predecessor simply carries on."""


def journal_for(target):
    """The journal a successor reads first — one per ADDRESS, never one per repo. An orchestrator gets its own
    file beside the planning session's: the two run in the same worktree, and a shared journal would hand each
    the other's handoff entry.

    ONE NAME, but only once something has actually written it. No prompt ever told an orch's own session where
    its running notes belonged (unlike a track's, which is handed `progress.md` outright), so at least one
    (lessons@lessons-site) kept a `-handover.md` of its own — `--brief` looked for the canonical `-handoff.md`,
    found nothing, and printed the journal section empty while 230+ kB of real history sat one word away. Never
    rename what is already on disk: prefer the canonical name, and fall back to the legacy `-handover.md` only
    when the canonical one does not exist — so a fresh address still gets `-handoff.md`, an address that is
    already keeping its journal under the old name keeps being read (and appended to) there, and the two never
    fork into two half-histories. BOTH SIDES resolve it, or the fork happens anyway: cc-context's
    journal_parts() applies the same two-name rule to the journal a session is told to write and to the
    worker's stop marker it appends. Change one and change the other."""
    repo, alias, track = parts(target)
    if track:
        return f"~/.cc/state/{repo}/{track}/progress.md"
    canon = f"~/.cc/state/{repo}/{alias or 'planning'}-handoff.md"
    legacy = f"~/.cc/state/{repo}/{alias or 'planning'}-handover.md"
    if not os.path.exists(os.path.expanduser(canon)) and os.path.exists(os.path.expanduser(legacy)):
        return legacy
    return canon


def logd(msg):
    """One line where a DETACHED child's output already goes — the only trace a process with no terminal leaves."""
    try:
        log = handoff_log()
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(log), exist_ok=True)
        with open(log, "a") as f:
            f.write("%s cc-handoff: %s\n" % (time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()), msg))
    except Exception:
        pass


PROMPT_LINE = re.compile(r"^[^\w>❯]*[>❯](.*)$")   # Claude Code's `>` (`❯` since 2.1) — a taken turn, or the input box
DIALOG_MARKS = ("Do you want to", "No, and tell Claude", "Esc to cancel", "Enter to confirm",
                "Would you like to proceed?")    # cc-msg's dialog guard, the same strings: keys typed now answer a modal
IDLE_HINT = "? for shortcuts"                    # under the input box while the session waits for a turn
WORKING = "esc to interrupt"                     # the footer for the whole of a turn (cc-reconcile's WORKING_FOOTER)


def screen(wid, history=0):
    """What the pane shows now — and, with `history`, that many lines above it too. Dialogs are judged on the
    visible screen only: a dialog pressed through hours ago is still in the scrollback."""
    return tmux("capture-pane", "-p", *(("-S", str(-history)) if history else ()), "-t", wid) or ""


def dialog_open(wid):
    """Is a startup confirmation up in that pane — Claude Code's development-channels warning, or the MCP trust
    prompt? Keys typed while one is up go INTO IT, not into the session behind it."""
    return any(s in screen(wid) for s in ("Enter to confirm", "development channels", "local development",
                                          "Use this MCP server"))


def idle(wid):
    """Is the session in that pane WAITING, with nothing typed? No working footer, no dialog, and the input box —
    the bottom-most prompt line — empty: the TUI draws `❯ ` and a non-breaking space, older ones a placeholder.
    A blank pane is not idle: nothing has been drawn to be idle at."""
    pane = screen(wid)
    if not pane.strip() or WORKING in pane or any(s in pane for s in DIALOG_MARKS):
        return False
    last = None
    for l in pane.splitlines():
        m = PROMPT_LINE.match(l)
        if m:
            last = m.group(1)
    if last is None:
        return False
    last = last.replace("\u00a0", " ").strip()
    return not last or last.startswith('Try "')


def prompt_rendered(wid):
    """Has the session drawn its input box? The pane is live the instant the shell forks cc, long before Claude
    Code draws anything — liveness says nothing about whether keys will reach a session."""
    pane = screen(wid)
    return IDLE_HINT in pane or any(PROMPT_LINE.match(l) for l in pane.splitlines())


def box_holds(pane, text):
    """Is the text still SITTING in the input box — typed, Enter not taken? The box is the bottom-most `>` line
    on screen (a taken turn's echo sits above it); an idle box is empty or shows a placeholder, never the text."""
    last = None
    for l in pane.splitlines():
        m = PROMPT_LINE.match(l)
        if m:
            last = m.group(1)
    return last is not None and "".join(text.split())[:12] in "".join(last.split())


def landed(wid, text):
    """Did the prompt reach the SESSION as a turn — not the dialog on top of it, and not the input box alone?
    Read the pane back (scrollback too: a quick first reply pushes the echo up) and compare with ALL whitespace
    removed, because the TUI wraps a typed line at the pane width. An echo proves the keys arrived; the turn is
    proven by the working footer, or by the text having LEFT the input box (the 03:32Z successor sat with the
    brief typed and untaken at an empty prompt)."""
    pane = screen(wid, history=120)
    if "".join(text.split())[:48] not in "".join(pane.split()):
        return False
    return WORKING in pane or not box_holds(pane, text)


KICK = (MACHINE + "{hid}: you are the SUCCESSOR for {target} — your handoff brief is in your system prompt. "
        "Start now: run `cc-handoff --brief {target}` first, ask the predecessor ONE batch of questions about "
        "what the packet did not answer, and run `cc-handoff --ready` as soon as they are answered — the "
        "overlap is short and costs two sessions for every minute it is open.")


def kick(target, hid=""):
    """--kick, detached by start(): put the opening prompt IN FRONT OF the successor, and CHECK IT LANDED.
    The brief rides in --append-system-prompt, which no session ever acts on by itself — with no first user
    turn a successor just sits there. On 2026-09-01 one sat blank for 25 minutes: the startup dialog ate the
    prompt typed at it, the window looked perfectly healthy, and nothing ever read the pane back. Liveness is
    no signal (the pane is live before anything is drawn), so type only once the dialog has shown AND cleared,
    or a prompt is on screen — pressing the dialog through from here too, because this path must not depend
    on the Slack layer being set up — then read the pane back, and keep sending until it is SEEN to land or
    the wait runs out; then tell the owner rather than leave a silent session sitting beside a live one."""
    win = target + NEXT
    wait = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_KICK_WAIT", 240)
    pause = 2 if wait else 0                       # a fixture sets the wait to 0 and gets no naps with it
    deadline = time.time() + wait
    text = KICK.format(hid=(" " + hid) if hid else "", target=target, journal=journal_for(target))
    seen = asked = False                           # seen: a dialog was on screen; asked: cc-slack is pressing it
    tries = 0
    while True:
        if not read(target):
            return 0                               # abandoned while we waited: nothing to brief
        wid = win_id(win)
        up = bool(wid) and pane_live(win)
        if up and dialog_open(wid):
            seen = True
            if not asked:
                run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-slack"), "accept-dialog", win], wait=False)
                asked = True                       # cc-slack watches that window for 3 min of its own accord
        elif up and (seen or tries or prompt_rendered(wid) or time.time() >= deadline):
            tries += 1                             # at the deadline with nothing recognisable drawn: one try, judged like any other
            run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-msg"), win, text])      # never types over a dialog: it answers a known-safe
                                                   # prompt, else spools (and collapses these retries into one line)
            for _ in range(5):                     # the TUI takes a moment to echo a turn
                time.sleep(pause)
                if landed(wid, text):
                    logd(f"{target}: the successor's opening prompt landed (try {tries})")
                    return host_cutover(target)
        if time.time() >= deadline:
            return kick_lost(target, f"its opening prompt did not land in {tries} tries" if tries
                             else "it never got past its startup dialog" if seen else "it never drew a prompt")
        time.sleep(pause)


def host_cutover(target):
    """The prompt landed. A successor that cannot run --ready itself — a member workspace's, inside its sandbox
    where the record is not bound — is cut over from HERE, the host's own detached child, as `--ready --forced`:
    the same commit point, the same derive, the same drain-then-retire for the old session. Any other successor
    reads its packet and runs --ready when it is ready; nothing here hurries it."""
    rec = read(target)
    if not rec or not rec.get("host_cutover"):
        return 0
    rc = ready(target, forced=True)
    logd(f"{target}: host cutover for the member successor — rc {rc}")
    return rc


def kick_lost(target, why):
    """A successor that never got its prompt is a session sitting silent beside a live one. Only the owner can
    unstick it, so it goes to #alerts and into the target's journal — the two places the next reader looks —
    and the record runs to its own deadline either way, so nothing is left half-owned."""
    line = (f"{target}: the successor came up but {why} — it is sitting blank in main:{target}{NEXT}; "
            f"brief it by hand (`cc-msg {target}{NEXT} …`) or `cc-handoff --abandon {target}`")
    logd("NO opening prompt — " + line)
    alert(target, "⚠️ " + line)
    journal(target, line)
    return 1


def journal(target, line):
    """One dated line in the target's own journal — the file every successor reads first."""
    try:
        p = os.path.expanduser(journal_for(target))
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p), exist_ok=True)
        with open(p, "a") as f:
            f.write("\n## %s — cc-handoff\n%s\n" % (time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%MZ", time.gmtime()), line))
    except Exception:
        return
    lib_touch(p)


def lib_touch(path):
    """INDEX-AT-WRITE (cc-lib): the entry just appended is searchable a minute later, not at the next sweep. It
    changes nothing decided here: exit code and output are discarded, a failure is one line in cc-lib's own
    misses log (`cc-lib ask` reports it as hook-misses), and a cc-lib that is not there is nothing at all.
    subprocess directly and not run(): the selfcheck stubs run(), and this case needs the real index."""
    lib = os.path.join(BIN, "cc-lib")
    if not os.access(lib, os.X_OK):
        return
    try:
        subprocess.run([lib, "index", "--touch", path], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=15)
    except Exception:
        pass


def member_workspace(repo):
    """Is ~/dev/<repo> one member's whole world? The marker `cc` reads (member_workspace there): a session of it
    runs inside `cc-sandbox member`, and so does its handoff successor (`cc __runnext`)."""
    return os.path.isfile(os.path.join(DEV, repo, ".cc", "member-workspace"))


HOST_CUTOVER_MSG = ("THIS IS A MEMBER WORKSPACE, so the host cut you over the moment this prompt landed: you ARE the "
                    "live session, and `cc-handoff --ready` is not yours to run (it cannot run inside the boundary — "
                    "do not try). Your predecessor is draining and retires within minutes, so anything you must ask "
                    "it goes in your first turn; its journal is the record. ")


def flight_line(repo, note="", host=False, box=False):
    """What the successor is told is unfinished RIGHT NOW. READ OFF THE LIVE WORKER TABLE — the same one --brief
    prints — and printed only when that table is non-empty: on 2026-09-16 the brief asserted "a background task
    is still running" beside a workers section that said (none), and the successor spent one of its one batch
    of questions on a phantom. The caller's own --in-flight text is kept verbatim as ITS word, labelled so, and
    never promoted to a fact. A member successor (`host`) is told first that the host has cut it over, because
    the brief around this slot says to run --ready, which it cannot."""
    live = live_workers(repo)
    out = HOST_CUTOVER_MSG if host else ""
    if live.strip():
        names = ", ".join(l.split(None, 1)[0] for l in live.splitlines() if l.strip())
        # APPENDED, never assigned: a member successor's cutover notice is already in `out`, and a live loop under
        # the repo must not cost it (review of #542 — it did, and that brief fell back to "run --ready").
        out += f"Unfinished RIGHT NOW, which a journal always omits: these loops are still running under {repo} — {names} — ask about them. "
    if note and box:
        out += f"THE BOX opened this overlap, not your predecessor: {note} "
    elif note:
        out += f"Your predecessor's own note when it opened this overlap: {note} "
    return out


def start(target, session_id="", flight="", box=False):
    """--overlap: the successor comes up beside the predecessor, muted and gated, with the same working
    directory. Refused while a record for this target is open — one successor at a time, never a chain.
    `box`: the sweep's rotation opened it (ROTATION) — the brief says so, and the ledger row carries why.
    A MEMBER WORKSPACE's successor boots inside a fresh `cc-sandbox member` (cc __runnext) — the boundary as the
    box has it NOW — and cannot run --ready from in there (the record is not bound inside), so the record says
    `host_cutover` and kick() cuts over for it once its opening prompt has landed (--replace is the spelling)."""
    if not TARGET_RE.match(target):
        print(f"cc-handoff: '{target}' is not a target ({SHAPES})", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if worker():
        print(f"cc-handoff: {WORKER_REFUSAL}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    old = read(target)
    if old:
        print(f"cc-handoff: {target} already has an overlap open "
              f"(phase {old.get('phase')}, live {live_side(old)}) — one successor at a time")
        return 1
    wid = win_id(target)
    if not wid:
        print(f"cc-handoff: no live window main:{target} — nothing to start alongside")
        return 1
    if worker_held(wid):
        print(f"cc-handoff: main:{target} reads as a headless worker's window — {WORKER_REFUSAL}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if win_id(target + NEXT):
        print(f"cc-handoff: main:{target}{NEXT} already exists with no record — clear it by hand first")
        return 1
    now = time.time()
    hid = uuid.uuid4().hex[:12]
    sid = str(uuid.uuid4())
    cap = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP_MAX", DEFAULT_MAX)    # once: the deadline and the brief must name the same number
    repo = parts(target)[0]
    rec = {"id": hid, "target": target, "phase": "overlap", "live": "predecessor",
           # the seat's own id off its live process when the caller has none: a planning seat or an orch never
           # has one, and without it its subagents are invisible to --brief, --ready and the stay (seat_sid)
           "predecessor": {"session_id": session_id or seat_sid(wid) or "", "tmux": wid, "window": target},
           "successor": {"session_id": sid, "tmux": "", "window": target + NEXT},
           "started": now, "deadline": now + cap,
           # …and for as long as that runs, nothing but the successor wakes the predecessor — see AND QUIET.
           "quiet": quiet_claim(target),
           "host_cutover": member_workspace(repo),   # a member successor cannot run --ready: kick() does, for it
           "pct_at_start": None, "told_owner": None, "phases": []}
    if box:
        rec["box"] = flight                       # the rotation's own reason, kept with the record (a reader of the file, and the selfcheck)
    ledger(rec, "overlap-started", flight if box else "")
    write(rec)          # before the window, never after: a successor whose record is missing must find itself gated
    quiet(target, rec["quiet"])     # after the record names it, never before: see quiet_claim()
    with open(brief_path(target), "w") as f:
        f.write(BRIEF.format(target=target, journal=journal_for(target), tmux=wid, mins=int(cap // 60),
                             flight=flight_line(repo, flight, rec["host_cutover"], box)))
    # The successor's OWN window name, carried into it: `cc __runnext` knows the target but not this suffix, so
    # it was accepting the startup dialog in the PREDECESSOR's window and its own sat on the development-channels
    # confirmation until a human noticed (2026-09-01 02:10Z, the third time this shape of bug has parked a session).
    cmd = "CC_HANDOFF_WINDOW=%s " % shlex.quote(target + NEXT)
    for v in ("CC_CLAUDE", "CC_NOTIFY_LOG_ONLY", "CC_NOTIFY_LOG", "CC_HANDOFF_DIR", "CC_CTX_RECORDS"):
        if os.environ.get(v):                     # the new window has the tmux server's env, not ours
            cmd += "%s=%s " % (v, shlex.quote(os.environ[v]))
    # `exec`: the pane's shell BECOMES cc, so claude is the pane's direct child — the one place cc-msg's
    # `pgrep -P` looks (forked, claude was a grandchild and every automatic kick was refused). __runnext's own
    # tail already ends in `exec bash`, so nothing is lost.
    cmd += "exec %s __runnext %s %s %s" % (shlex.quote(os.path.join(BIN, "cc")), shlex.quote(target), hid, sid)
    if tmux("new-window", "-d", "-t", SESSION, "-n", target + NEXT, cmd) is None:
        clear(target)                             # nothing came up: leave no record claiming there is a successor
        print(f"cc-handoff: could not start main:{target}{NEXT} — no overlap opened", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    rec["successor"]["tmux"] = win_id(target + NEXT) or ""
    write(rec)
    # Detached, because the successor takes a minute to boot and the caller must not wait: it waits out the
    # startup dialog, sends the opening prompt and verifies the pane took it. Without it the successor has a
    # brief and no first turn — a window that looks right and a session doing nothing.
    if os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF_NO_KICK"):   # a suite's fixture window never boots a claude: a kick there can only
        logd(f"kick skipped for {target} (CC_HANDOFF_NO_KICK)")   # end in kick_lost, which pages — and tests must not
    else:
        run([os.path.realpath(__file__), "--kick", target, hid], wait=False)
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — successor started in main:{target}{NEXT} (id {hid}); "
          + ("the host cuts over for it once its opening prompt lands, then the old session drains and retires"
             if rec["host_cutover"] else "it is muted and gated until it runs `cc-handoff --ready`"))
    return 0


def replace(target, session_id=""):
    """--replace <workspace>: a LIVE member session is handed off onto the boundary the box has now — the one a
    landing just changed (units.json, the bwrap args: #453's core/mail mount reached no session that was already
    running, and the owner's test waited on a hand relaunch). It is the overlap, and nothing else: the successor
    comes up first, inside a fresh sandbox, reads the journal, and the old session drains and retires after the
    host's cutover — no window is killed under a session. Only a member workspace: any other kind runs --ready
    itself, so --overlap is its spelling."""
    if kind(target) != "session" or not member_workspace(target):
        print(f"cc-handoff: --replace is for a member workspace's session (<repo>, with "
              f"~/dev/<repo>/.cc/member-workspace); {target} is not one — `cc-handoff --overlap` hands off "
              f"a session that can run --ready itself", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    return start(target, session_id)


def ready(target=None, forced=False):
    """--ready: run BY THE SUCCESSOR, because the session that knows whether the transfer worked is the one
    that received it. Preflight, then the single os.replace(), then derive."""
    hid = os.environ.get("CC_HANDOFF") or ""
    rec = read(target) if target else (by_id(hid) if hid else None)
    if not rec:
        print(f"cc-handoff: no open overlap for {target or 'this session'}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    target = rec["target"]
    if live_side(rec) == "successor":
        derive(rec)                                # idempotent: a retried --ready re-derives, never rolls back
        print(f"cc-handoff: {target} already cut over")
        return 0
    if not forced and hid != rec["id"]:
        print("cc-handoff: --ready belongs to the successor (its CC_HANDOFF must match the record); "
              "--forced cuts over by hand", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if forced and not pane_live(rec["successor"]["window"]):
        print(f"cc-handoff: {target} has no live successor to force onto", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    # Is the predecessor being told to STAY for its subagents? Decided HERE, once, and carried down with the
    # commit write, because --retire runs in another process and a retried --ready must not take a second
    # answer — nor write a second journal line, nor send a second STAY_MSG (`first` below).
    pre = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""
    first = rec.get("held") is None
    # "--ready no longer silently ends a predecessor that is still waiting on work it named" (the brief, 2026-09-22):
    # the id is read off the live pane when the record has none, so a planning seat's or an orch's subagents hold it.
    if first:
        sid = pre_sid(rec) if pane_live_id(pre) else ""
        if sid:
            rec["predecessor"]["session_id"] = sid     # stay() reads its subagents by this id, in another process
        rec["held"] = subagents(sid) if sid else []
    held = rec.get("held") or []
    wk = dispatched(target)
    rec["live"] = "successor"                      # ---- THE COMMIT POINT ----
    rec["phase"] = "cutover"
    rec["cutover"] = time.time()
    staying = f"the predecessor is staying for {len(held)} subagent(s)" if held else "the predecessor is retiring"
    ledger(rec, "overlap-forced" if forced else "overlap-cutover")
    write(rec)
    derive(rec, first)
    tell_slack(target)                             # after the names move: the successor is live, so it is delivered to
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — the successor is live; " + (f"{staying} ({', '.join(held[:6])}), then retires"
          if held else "the predecessor is journalling, then retiring"))
    if wk:
        print(f"cc-handoff: {len(wk)} worker(s) your predecessor dispatched keep running and report to you: "
              f"{', '.join(wk[:6])}")
    if first and pane_live_id(pre) and not pre_sid(rec):
        print(f"cc-handoff: the predecessor's session id could not be read, so its subagents could not be counted "
              f"— if it named any it was waiting on, ask it before it retires", file=sys.stderr)
    return 0


def carry_marks(target, pre, suc):
    """THE ROWS THE PREDECESSOR WAS WORKING IN ITS OWN SUBAGENTS COME WITH THE HANDOFF (2026-09-07). Such a row
    has no worker, no cc-loop and no window of its own, so the only thing on the box saying anybody owns it is
    the `agent` mark that session stamped on it — its own window, `<window id>:<tmux server pid>` (cc-board
    agent_id) — and a reader counts that mark only while the window it names is a session named for the repo.
    Cutover renames the predecessor to <target>~old, so every mark it made stops counting in the same instant,
    while its subagents are DELIBERATELY still running (THE PREDECESSOR STAYS FOR ITS SUBAGENTS, above) and
    reporting into the successor. Ten minutes later the watchdog called five such rows abandoned and blocked
    them, and a blocked row is offered for dispatch again — the setup for two workers on one file. So the marks
    move where the work reports: the successor owns the channel, takes those reports and finishes those rows.

    Derived, so it sits after the commit point with the rest, and idempotent by construction — a second run finds
    no row carrying the predecessor's mark. Returns the tracks that moved.

    THE TWO ROWS IT MUST NEVER TOUCH. A mark that is not EXACTLY the predecessor's belongs to a third session and
    is none of this handoff's business (`@1:900` is not `@12:900`, which is why cc-board matches the whole mark
    and not a prefix). And a row a real worker is in is that worker's — asked the way the watchdog asks it, a
    live pane in the track's own window `<repo>/<track>` — so this changes nothing about work it is not retiring.
    Everything else fails closed: no window id either side, no answer from tmux, no board, and nothing moves."""
    repo = parts(target)[0]
    if not pre or not suc or pre == suc:
        return []
    # The successor's mark, in the shape cc-board's `agent me` writes — so what lands on the row is exactly
    # what that session would have stamped itself. The shape is all that is borrowed: `agent me` also refuses
    # a window no reader counts, and this call runs after the rename above has given `suc` the name <target>,
    # which is the name a reader counts. The server pid is the same for both windows (one server; a different
    # one means both sessions are already gone), so it also gives the predecessor's mark.
    got = (tmux("display-message", "-p", "-t", suc, "#{window_id}:#{pid}") or "").strip()
    wid, _, spid = got.partition(":")
    if wid != suc or not spid.isdigit():
        return []
    frm, to = f"{pre}:{spid}", got
    board = os.path.join(BIN, "cc-board")
    try:
        rows = (json.loads(out([board, "json", repo], fail="") or "{}") or {}).get("tracks") or {}
    except Exception:
        return []
    mine = [t for t in sorted(rows) if (rows[t].get("agent") or "").strip() == frm
            and not pane_live(f"{repo}/{t}")]
    if not mine:
        return []
    return (out([board, "handover", repo, frm, to, *mine], fail="") or "").split()


def derive(rec, first=False):
    """Everything after the commit point: idempotent, retried, never rolled back. `first` says this call is the
    one that took the cutover — a retry re-derives the cosmetics but never re-announces a stay. A failure here leaves the
    record right about WHO IS LIVE and wrong only about cosmetics, and a later turn re-derives the same state."""
    target = rec["target"]
    repo, _, track = parts(target)
    sid = (rec.get("successor") or {}).get("session_id")
    if track and sid:
        board = os.path.join(BIN, "cc-board")
        run([board, "set", repo, track, "session_id", sid])
        run([board, "set", repo, track, "started", "true"])
        run([board, "note", repo, track, "handoff: the overlapping successor took over"])
    # THE NAMES MOVE NOW, not at the end of the drain: an owner attaching to main:<target>, and a `cc-msg <target>`,
    # must reach the live session from the first instant after the commit point. By id — a window renamed by
    # hand is still that session — and the predecessor first, which is what frees the name.
    pre = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""
    suc = (rec.get("successor") or {}).get("tmux") or win_id(target + NEXT) or ""   # no id on the record: by its startup name
    old = target + OLD
    # ONE tmux call for both: between two, no window is `<target>`, and a `cc <repo>` in that instant starts a third session.
    cmds = ["rename-window", "-t", pre, old] if win_alive(pre) else []
    if win_alive(suc):
        cmds += ([";"] if cmds else []) + ["rename-window", "-t", suc, target]
    if cmds:
        tmux(*cmds)
    # …and the quiet marks come off with the name. `<target>` is the SUCCESSOR's from here, and it is not
    # retiring: leaving pulse.off on it would take the new session out of the drive loop for good.
    unquiet(rec)
    # kick() spooled the opening prompt under the successor's STARTUP name (<target>~next); the rename above would
    # strand that mail forever (review-157b) — it moves with the window, appended behind anything already spooled
    # for the live name, and the ~next state dir (only ever cc-msg inbox files; `~` is not legal in a name) goes too.
    nxt = os.path.join(STATE, target + NEXT)
    nsp = os.path.join(nxt, "inbox.spool")
    if os.path.exists(nsp):
        tdir = os.path.join(STATE, target)
        os.makedirs(tdir, exist_ok=True)
        # UNDER CC-MSG'S OWN LOCK. Its 60 s drain rewrites <target>/inbox.spool as `tail -n +2 > tmp && mv`, so an
        # append landing between that tail and its mv is replaced by the mv — and the rmtree below then deletes the
        # only other copy, which is the exact stranding this hunk exists to prevent. Same file, same .inbox.lock.
        with open(os.path.join(tdir, ".inbox.lock"), "w") as lk:
            fcntl.flock(lk, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
            with open(nsp) as src, open(os.path.join(tdir, "inbox.spool"), "a") as dst:
                dst.write(src.read())
            shutil.rmtree(nxt, ignore_errors=True)     # inside the lock: nothing is deleted that is not yet copied
    shutil.rmtree(nxt, ignore_errors=True)             # …and the ~next dir goes whether or not it held mail
    if (rec["predecessor"].get("window"), rec["successor"].get("window"), rec["successor"].get("tmux")) != (old, target, suc):
        rec["predecessor"]["window"], rec["successor"]["window"], rec["successor"]["tmux"] = old, target, suc
        write(rec)
    # …and the board rows the predecessor was holding in its own subagents move with the name — see carry_marks().
    # After the rename, because that is the moment the marks stop counting, and a retried derive() moves none.
    moved = carry_marks(target, pre, suc)
    if moved:
        journal(target, f"handoff: {len(moved)} board row(s) the retiring session held in a subagent are the "
                        f"successor's now ({', '.join(moved[:6])}) — the watchdog would have called them "
                        f"abandoned ten minutes after cutover.")
    # THE PREDECESSOR STAYS FOR ITS SUBAGENTS (owner, 2026-09-04) — ready() decided it and the record carries
    # the answer. Said once: a retried --ready re-derives everything here, and a session told to stay is not
    # told twice, nor sent the retirement line it is not on yet.
    held = rec.get("held") or []
    if held and first:
        journal(target, f"handoff: the successor is live and owns {target}; this session stays for the "
                        f"{len(held)} subagent(s) still running ({', '.join(held[:6])}), relays each report to "
                        f"it, and retires when the last one is in.")
    if pane_live_id(pre) and (first or not held):
        run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-msg"), old, STAY_MSG.format(target=target, old=old, n=len(held)) if held
             else RETIRE_MSG.format(target=target, old=old)])
    run([os.path.realpath(__file__), "--retire", target], wait=False)


RETIRE_MSG = (MACHINE + " Handoff complete: your successor is live and owns this target now — it holds window "
              "{target}; you are {old} and retiring. Append your final entry to the journal — what is done, what "
              "is next (ordered), gotchas — and stop. You are no longer the live session: merging, pushing, "
              "dispatching and posting outward are refused from here on, and this window ends by itself once "
              "you are idle.")
STAY_MSG = (MACHINE + " Handoff cut over: your successor is live and owns this target now — it holds window "
            "{target}, you are {old}, and you are NOT finished. {n} of your subagents are still running and "
            "their reports have nowhere else to land, so STAY: watch them through, and hand each report on to "
            "your successor as it comes in — `cc-msg {target} '<the report, in a line or two>'`, or SendMessage "
            "if you hold it as a peer — because it is the one who will act on it. Start nothing new: merging, "
            "pushing, dispatching and posting outward are refused from here on. When the last one is in you get "
            "the final handoff message; then journal and stop. This window ends by itself once your subagents "
            "are done. The board rows those subagents are "
            "on are marked to {target} now — leave them alone: `cc board set … agent me` is REFUSED from here, "
            "because this window is one no reader counts and the mark would be the same as releasing them.")


def refuse_kill(rec, wid):
    """THE ONE GENUINELY DANGEROUS ACT IN THIS FILE. Killing the wrong window ends a working session and loses
    a context nobody can get back, so the kill is fenced by three refusals and each says which one it was.
    A caller that gets a reason back must kill NOTHING."""
    if not wid:
        return "there is no window left to end"
    if wid != ((rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""):
        return (f"tmux window {wid} is not the one the record names as the predecessor "
                f"({(rec.get('predecessor') or {}).get('tmux') or 'none'})")
    if wid == ((rec.get("successor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""):
        return "the record names the same window on both sides"
    if wid == self_wid():
        return "that is the window --retire is running in"
    return ""


def stay(rec, pre):
    """The predecessor was told to STAY: hold its window — no drain, no kill — while any of its subagents is
    still running, then send it the FINAL handoff message and hand back a drain clock starting NOW, so its last
    journal entry is written after the last report and not before it. Bounded by CC_HANDOFF_AGENT_MAX: a stay
    expires into the same drain-then-kill as everything else here, never into "it keeps its window". It also
    ends the moment that session is gone — there is then nobody left to relay a report."""
    target = rec["target"]
    sid = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("session_id") or ""
    idle, cap, t0 = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_AGENT_IDLE", 1800), cfg("CC_HANDOFF_AGENT_MAX", 3600), time.time()
    left = rec.get("held") or []
    while pane_live_id(pre):
        left = subagents(sid, idle)
        if not left or time.time() >= t0 + cap:
            break
        time.sleep(15)
    mins = int((time.time() - t0) / 60)
    if not pane_live_id(pre):
        why = f"its session ended first, {mins} min in"
    elif left:
        why = f"the wait ran out after {mins} min with {len(left)} still running ({', '.join(left[:6])})"
    else:
        why = f"its last subagent finished after {mins} min"
    journal(target, f"handoff: the predecessor stayed for its subagents — {why}; it takes the final handoff "
                    f"message now and retires.")
    logd(f"{target}: the stay for the predecessor's subagents is over — {why}")
    now = time.time()
    fresh = read(target)                           # never re-create a record cleared under us: only stamp a live one
    if fresh and live_side(fresh) == "successor":
        fresh["held"] = []                         # decided and over — not absent, which a retried --ready would re-decide
        fresh["drain_from"] = now
        write(fresh)
    old = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("window") or (target + OLD)
    if pane_live_id(pre):
        run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-msg"), old, RETIRE_MSG.format(target=target, old=old)])
    return now


def drain_start(rec, amax):
    """When the predecessor's drain clock starts: at cutover — except that a predecessor told to STAY for its
    subagents keeps its window past the drain, so until the stay ends (drain_from) --sweep must wait out the
    stay's own cap as well. Without this the sweep ends the session that was told to stay, twelve minutes in."""
    return rec.get("drain_from") or (rec.get("cutover") or 0) + (amax if rec.get("held") else 0)


def retire(target):
    """--retire, detached by derive(): DRAIN the predecessor, then END it — every time, not only when a human
    happened to be watching (a session cannot exit itself; on 2026-08-31 a retired one ran on for 35 min as a
    second live voice). The window lives on — renamed `<target>~old` at cutover — for CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN, or
    until it is idle with nothing typed, never less than CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE (the turn in flight, the
    journal flushed). A predecessor staying for its subagents waits them out before any of that begins.
    Then the record says `retired` and the window is killed.
    Kills ONLY the window the record names as the predecessor, ONLY once the record says the successor is live,
    and never the caller's own — see refuse_kill(). No record, or no cutover: nothing is killed at all."""
    rec = read(target)
    if not rec or live_side(rec) != "successor":
        return 0                                   # missing, corrupt, or before cutover: kill NOTHING
    pre = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""
    grace, drain = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE", 120), cfg("CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN", 600)
    # A predecessor holding live subagents was told to stay: it is waited out FIRST, and the final handoff
    # message it gets at the end of that is what starts the drain — see stay().
    t0 = stay(rec, pre) if rec.get("held") else (rec.get("cutover") or time.time())
    quiet = 0
    while pane_live_id(pre):                       # a session that exited leaves a bare shell: nothing left to drain
        now = time.time()
        if now >= t0 + drain:
            break
        quiet = quiet + 1 if now >= t0 + grace and idle(pre) else 0
        if quiet >= 2:                             # two reads apart: one idle glimpse mid-typing is not "nothing queued"
            break
        time.sleep(5)
    rec = read(target)
    if not rec or live_side(rec) != "successor":
        return 0                                   # finalized or cleared under us: nothing here is ours to end
    if rec.get("phase") != "retired":
        rec["phase"] = "retired"                   # the drain is over
        write(rec)
    wid = pre if win_alive(pre) else ""            # by the id the record names: `<target>` is the SUCCESSOR's name now
    if wid:
        why = refuse_kill(rec, wid)
        if why:                                    # the record and tmux disagree: the record stays, and so does the gate
            print(f"cc-handoff: {target}'s predecessor was NOT ended — {why}. It keeps its window and "
                  f"everything stays gated; this needs a hand.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        tmux("kill-window", "-t", wid)
    nid = win_id(target + NEXT)                    # a successor still on its startup name: a cutover older than this code
    if nid and not win_id(target):
        tmux("rename-window", "-t", nid, target)
    # mail addressed to the retired window dies with it: RETIRE_MSG may have spooled under <target>~old, and a
    # spool whose window is gone would otherwise stand as junk and be reported as stranded (review-157b)
    shutil.rmtree(os.path.join(STATE, target + OLD), ignore_errors=True)
    rec["phase"] = "done"
    ledger(rec, "overlap-retired")
    write(rec)
    mark_done(rec)                                 # before clear(), always: it is what tells cc-guard this went RIGHT
    clear(target)                                  # nobody is gated any more: the successor is the only session left
    tell_slack(target)
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — the predecessor is ended, the successor has its window, nothing is gated")
    return 0


def abandon(target, why=""):
    """--abandon: call the overlap off. The successor's window goes, the record clears, the owner hears it
    ONCE — the record's absence is what makes a second call a no-op. Never two sessions forever."""
    rec = read(target)
    if not rec:
        return 0
    if live_side(rec) == "successor":
        print(f"cc-handoff: {target} has already cut over — nothing to abandon", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    wid = side_wid(rec, "successor")
    if wid:
        tmux("kill-window", "-t", wid)
    rec["told_owner"] = time.time()
    ledger(rec, "overlap-abandoned", why)
    clear(target)
    notify(target, f"Overlapping handoff abandoned for {target}: {why or 'the successor never became ready'}. "
                   f"The original session is still running and still in charge.")
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — overlap abandoned, the predecessor carries on")
    return 0


def expire(rec, why):
    """An overlap that ends with no cutover: the successor's window goes, the record clears, and the PREDECESSOR is
    told in its own pane and journal. It is still live and was waiting on the successor, so an expiry it never
    hears of is an hour of work past its hand-off line and a second successor started by hand (2026-09-24, d861dd1f3971,
    and twice before that). The owner is not told: nothing is broken that the seat cannot restart itself."""
    target = rec["target"]
    wid = side_wid(rec, "successor")
    if wid:
        tmux("kill-window", "-t", wid)
    ledger(rec, "overlap-expired", why)
    clear(target)
    line = (f"{MACHINE} Your handoff {rec.get('id', '')} is over with no cutover: {why}. You are still the live "
            f"session for {target}. Hand off again with `cc-handoff --overlap {target}` when you are ready.")
    run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-msg"), target, line])
    journal(target, line)
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — the overlap expired without a cutover ({why}); the predecessor carries on and was told")


def finalize(rec, why):
    """A retirement nobody is left to finish. mark_done() BEFORE clear(), always: that order is the only reason
    the survivor of a completed handoff is not gated by the record of its own success."""
    target = rec["target"]
    pre = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""
    # The predecessor's SESSION is gone; its window may not be — a session that exited leaves a bare shell that
    # still holds `<target>`, so the rename below never happened and six successors sat on as `~next` with no
    # record (2026-09-01 05:15Z). An empty shell is not a session: it goes, under the same fence as a kill.
    if win_alive(pre) and not pane_live_id(pre) and not refuse_kill(rec, pre):
        tmux("kill-window", "-t", pre)
    nid = side_wid(rec, "successor")
    if nid and not win_id(target):                 # the predecessor is gone: its name is free
        tmux("rename-window", "-t", nid, target)
    rec["phase"] = "done"
    ledger(rec, "overlap-swept", why)
    write(rec)
    mark_done(rec)
    clear(target)
    repo, _, track = parts(target)
    if track:
        run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-board"), "note", repo, track, f"handoff: swept — {why}"])
    print(f"cc-handoff: {target} — {why}; the record is finalized and nothing is gated by it")


# ---- rotation: the box hands off a seat past the rotate line (ROTATION in the header) ------------------

SID_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$")   # a session id on a command line: the shape cc-reconcile's seat_turn accepts


def seat_of(wid):
    """The SEAT in that window: (session id or "", cwd, started) of the interactive `claude` under its pane, or
    None when there is none. The id is the `--session-id` (a track seat, a successor) or `--resume` (a track
    brought back) on its command line; a planning seat (`cc <repo>`) and an orch (`cc --orch`) never carry one,
    nor does a `-c` track — those are known by their cwd. `started` is the live process's own start (the epoch
    /proc/<pid> was created), which is what bounds a measurement taken by cwd: see measure_seat().
    A `-p`/`--print` claude is a worker's iteration, not a seat."""
    k = pane_claude(wid)
    if not k:
        return None
    try:
        argv = open(f"/proc/{k}/cmdline", "rb").read().decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0")
        cwd = os.readlink(f"/proc/{k}/cwd")
        started = os.stat(f"/proc/{k}").st_ctime    # when THIS process started, in epoch seconds
    except OSError:
        return None
    sid = ""
    for flag in ("--session-id", "--resume"):
        i = argv.index(flag) if flag in argv else -1
        if 0 <= i < len(argv) - 1 and SID_RE.match(argv[i + 1]):
            sid = argv[i + 1]
    return sid, cwd, started


def pane_claude(wid):
    """The pid of the interactive `claude` under that window's pane, through any wrapper (cc, cc-sandbox, bwrap),
    or "" — a `-p`/`--print` claude is a worker's iteration, not a seat."""
    todo, seen = (tmux("list-panes", "-t", wid, "-F", "#{pane_pid}") or "").split() if wid else [], set()
    while todo:
        pid = todo.pop()
        if pid in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(pid)
        try:
            kids = open(f"/proc/{pid}/task/{pid}/children").read().split()
        except OSError:
            kids = []
        for k in kids:
            try:
                if open(f"/proc/{k}/comm").read().strip() != "claude":
                    todo.append(k)                 # a wrapper (cc, cc-sandbox, bwrap): the seat is under it
                    continue
                argv = open(f"/proc/{k}/cmdline", "rb").read().decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0")
            except OSError:
                continue
            if any(a in ("-p", "--print") for a in argv):
                continue
            return k
    return ""


def measure_seat(target, sid, alone, since=0):
    """cc-context's --json for that seat, or None when it cannot be measured. By its session id when it has
    one; a track with none by its address (the board's id, else its worktree's transcript); any other seat with
    none by its checkout (`cc-context <repo>`, the newest session there) and ONLY when it is alone in it — a
    planning seat and an orch share one checkout, and the newest file there says nothing about which is fat.
    rc 2 (no transcript cc-context can read: a member workspace's lives outside ~/.claude/projects) is None.
    THE CHECKOUT IS MEASURED ONLY FROM `since`, THE SEAT'S OWN START (`cc-context --since`): what that checkout
    holds is the newest session file inside a DAY, so a seat the owner started after quitting a 300k one was
    measured on the dead session's transcript and rotated before its first turn (review of #552). A seat with
    nothing written since it started is unmeasurable, which is to say left alone."""
    repo, alias, track = parts(target)
    if sid:
        argv = ["--session", sid]
    elif track:
        argv = [repo, track]
    elif alone:
        argv = [repo] + (["--since", str(int(since))] if since else [])
    else:
        return None
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-context")] + argv + ["--json"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=90)
        m = json.loads(r.stdout) if r.returncode == 0 else None
    except Exception:
        return None
    return m if isinstance(m, dict) and isinstance(m.get("used"), (int, float)) else None


def rotate():
    """--sweep --rotate's second half — ROTATION in the header. Every live window in `main` named like a target,
    with no record open, no `~next` beside it and not a worker's, is measured by the seat in it; one past the
    rotate line gets the ordinary overlap opened for it, once per CC_HANDOFF_ROTATE_RETRY. Returns how many it
    opened. PEERS ARE COUNTED FIRST, over every live window: a planning seat and its orch share one checkout and
    neither has an id, so "alone" must see the orch even when the orch is filtered out below (its overlap open,
    its `~next` up, a worker misread) — counted after the filters, a 50k planning seat was rotated on its orch
    predecessor's 300k number (review of #552)."""
    retry, now = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_ROTATE_RETRY", 3600), time.time()
    seats, peers = [], {}                          # (target, window id, session id, cwd, start); cwd -> live seats there, any window
    for l in (tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id} #{pane_pid} #W") or "").splitlines():
        col = l.split(" ", 2)
        if len(col) < 3:
            continue
        wid, _, name = col
        if not pane_live_id(wid):
            continue                               # no session in it
        seat = seat_of(wid)
        if not seat:
            continue
        peers[seat[1]] = peers.get(seat[1], 0) + 1
        if not TARGET_RE.match(name):
            continue                               # a side of a handoff or an unaddressable window: a peer, never a candidate
        if read(name) or win_id(name + NEXT):
            continue                               # an overlap is open, or a successor window nobody recorded (orphans() reports it)
        if worker_held(wid):
            continue                               # a loop's window: its iteration is its own handoff (WORKER_REFUSAL)
        seats.append((name, wid) + seat)
    opened = skilled = 0
    for target, wid, sid, cwd, started in seats:
        stamp = os.path.join(HANDOFF, slug(target) + ".rotated")
        try:
            if now - os.path.getmtime(stamp) < retry:
                continue                           # the box opened one inside the retry window: expired or not, not again yet
        except OSError:
            pass
        m = measure_seat(target, sid, peers.get(cwd, 0) == 1, started)
        # "a seat over the hard line is handed off ... a seat under the line is untouched" (the brief): cc-context's
        # `rotate` is that judgement, and nothing else here reads a number
        if not m or not m.get("rotate"):
            if skilled < cfg("CC_HANDOFF_SKILL_MAX", 2):
                skilled += skill_turn(target, wid, sid, cwd, started, m)
            continue
        why = (f"this seat was at {int(m['used']) // 1000}k tokens, past the {int(m['rotate_line']) // 1000}k rotate "
               f"line, and the box hands a seat off there rather than pay its whole context on every wake; "
               f"its journal is the packet, so ask it only what the journal lacks.")
        rc = start(target, sid, why, box=True)
        logd(f"rotation: {target} at {int(m['used'])} tokens (line {int(m['rotate_line'])}) — start rc {rc}")
        if rc != 0:
            continue                               # refused (no window, a worker's caller): no stamp, so the next tick tries again
        try:                                       # the stamp says an overlap OPENED — written only once one has
            os.makedirs(HANDOFF, exist_ok=True)
            with open(stamp, "w") as f:
                json.dump({"target": target, "session_id": sid, "used": m["used"], "ts": now}, f)
        except OSError:
            pass
        journal(target, f"rotated by the box: {why}")
        opened += 1
    return opened + skilled


def skill_names():
    """The skills a member workspace sees: `cc-skills names`, the list cc-sandbox binds one by one. None when it
    cannot be read, and then a member seat counts no host skill as its own."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-skills"), "names"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=30)
        return set(r.stdout.split()) if r.returncode == 0 else None
    except Exception:
        return None


def skill_changes(cwd, since, member=False):
    """{name: when} for every skill a seat in `cwd` loads whose SKILL.md arrived or changed after `since` (the seat's
    start) — SKILL CHANGES in the header. The roots are the host's SKILLS and the checkout's own .claude/skills; a
    member seat counts only the host skills its sandbox binds (skill_names). `when` is the later of the file's mtime
    and ctime, so a skill copied in with its old mtime kept still reads as new. Dot names are half clones, never a skill."""
    names = skill_names() if member else None
    out = {}
    for root in [SKILLS] + ([os.path.join(cwd, ".claude", "skills")] if cwd else []):
        try:
            ents = os.listdir(root)
        except OSError:
            continue
        for n in ents:
            if n.startswith(".") or (member and root == SKILLS and n not in (names or ())):
                continue
            try:
                st = os.stat(os.path.join(root, n, "SKILL.md"))
            except OSError:
                continue
            t = max(st.st_mtime, st.st_ctime)
            if t > since:
                out[n] = max(out.get(n, 0), t)
    return out


def activity(wid):
    """When that window last drew anything (tmux's window_activity, epoch seconds), 0 when unreadable."""
    try:
        return float(tmux("display-message", "-p", "-t", wid, "#{window_activity}") or 0)
    except ValueError:
        return 0


RELOAD_CMD = "/reload-skills"
_reloads = {}


def can_reload(pid):
    """Does the claude binary behind that pid know RELOAD_CMD? Read once per binary (its realpath), by looking for
    the command's registered name in it. A pid whose binary cannot be read (gone, or behind a sandbox) counts as
    able: every version installed on this box is, and a reload typed at one that is not only prints an error."""
    try:
        exe = os.path.realpath(f"/proc/{pid}/exe")
        if exe not in _reloads:
            with open(exe, "rb") as f:
                _reloads[exe] = f'"{RELOAD_CMD[1:]}"'.encode() in f.read()
        return _reloads[exe]
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return True


def skill_turn(target, wid, sid, cwd, started, m):
    """The skill half of rotate(): at a seat's first idle moment after a turn it took since its skill set changed,
    type RELOAD_CMD at it, or open the ordinary overlap where its binary has none. 1 when it did either, else 0."""
    stamp = os.path.join(HANDOFF, slug(target) + ".skills")
    try:
        with open(stamp) as f:
            last_done = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        last_done = {}
    if last_done.get("how") == "handoff" and time.time() - last_done.get("ts", 0) < cfg("CC_HANDOFF_ROTATE_RETRY", 3600):
        return 0                                   # opened one inside the retry window: expired or not, not again yet
    since = max(started, last_done.get("ts", 0) if last_done.get("how") == "reload" and last_done.get("session_id") == sid else 0)
    ch = skill_changes(cwd, since, member_workspace(parts(target)[0]))
    if not ch:
        return 0                                   # "hand off only sessions whose skill set actually changed" (the brief)
    newest = max(ch.values())
    try:                                           # the seat's last turn: its transcript, else what its pane last drew
        last = os.path.getmtime(m["transcript"]) if m and m.get("transcript") else activity(wid)
    except OSError:
        last = activity(wid)
    if last <= newest:
        return 0                                   # no turn since the change: a dormant seat waits until it is used
    if not idle(wid):
        return 0                                   # "when each next goes idle, never mid-turn" (the brief): next tick
    names = ", ".join(sorted(ch))
    # "prefer typing a skills-reload command into an idle session over a full handoff ... use a handoff only where
    # a reload is not possible" (the owner's addition to the brief)
    if can_reload(pane_claude(wid)):
        how = "reload"
        ok = run([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-msg"), target, RELOAD_CMD])
        logd(f"skills: {target} ({names}) — {RELOAD_CMD} {'typed' if ok else 'not typed'}")
        note = f"the box typed {RELOAD_CMD} for changed skills: {names}"
    else:
        how = "handoff"
        why = (f"the skills this seat loads changed after it started ({names}), its claude has no {RELOAD_CMD}, and a "
               f"session reads its skills only at start, so the box hands it off at its first idle moment; its journal "
               f"is the packet, so ask it only what the journal lacks.")
        rc = start(target, sid, why, box=True)
        logd(f"skills: {target} ({names}) — start rc {rc}")
        ok = rc == 0
        note = f"handed off by the box for new skills: {names}"
    if not ok:
        return 0                                   # not typed or refused: no stamp, so the next tick tries again
    try:
        os.makedirs(HANDOFF, exist_ok=True)
        with open(stamp, "w") as f:
            json.dump({"target": target, "session_id": sid, "skills": sorted(ch), "how": how, "ts": time.time()}, f)
    except OSError:
        pass
    journal(target, note)
    return 1


def sweep(rotate_too=False):
    """--sweep: the reaper, run from the EXISTING cc-reconcile timer (no new unit); `--rotate` (rotate_too)
    adds ROTATION, and only the tick's --apply passes it — a bare `cc-reconcile` reports and opens nothing.
    Nothing polices a record from inside a session, so the sweep IS the deadline, and it finishes what no
    session is left to:
      the predecessor's session is GONE past the retire grace -> finalize. Nobody but the successor is in
        charge of that target, and a record saying otherwise is gating the only session left.
      an overlap that never reached --ready is past its deadline (plus the grace), or its successor's session is
        gone two sweeps apart -> it expires (expire): the owner is not told, but the predecessor is, in its pane
        and its journal, because it is waiting on a successor that will never come.
      a cut-over predecessor STILL ALIVE past drain+grace -> ended here, then finalized (one told to STAY for
        its subagents is past nothing until that stay ends or its cap runs out: drain_start). The --retire that
        should have ended it died, or refused because a --forced cutover runs it from the predecessor's own pane
        (refuse_kill); left alone it is `~old` forever at phase retired, and its successor's <id>.done is never
        written. Same fence as --retire; the sweep has no pane of its own.
    A sweep only ever un-gates, so a WRONG 'gone' is the expensive direction: if tmux cannot be read at all —
    no server, no session main — every window looks gone, so nothing is swept."""
    if tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id}") is None:
        return 0
    grace, drain = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_RETIRE_GRACE", 120), cfg("CC_HANDOFF_DRAIN", 600)
    amax, now = cfg("CC_HANDOFF_AGENT_MAX", 3600), time.time()
    for rec in read_all():
        target = rec["target"]
        if not TARGET_RE.match(target):
            continue                               # a record naming something that is not a target: not ours to act on
        pred_live = side_live(rec, "predecessor")
        if pred_live and rec.get("gone_since"):
            rec.pop("gone_since", None)            # it came back: a misread, which is exactly what the stamp absorbs
            write(rec)
        if not pred_live and now - (rec.get("cutover") or rec.get("started") or 0) > grace:
            # TWO STRIKES, a sweep apart. One transient tmux/pgrep misread here would un-gate the successor while
            # the predecessor keeps working — two un-gated voices, the 2026-08-31 incident class. The first sweep
            # that finds nobody only writes down WHEN; the finalize needs a later sweep still finding nobody.
            gone = rec.get("gone_since") or 0
            if not gone:
                rec["gone_since"] = now
                write(rec)
            elif now - gone >= cfg("CC_HANDOFF_GONE_CONFIRM", 300):
                finalize(rec, "the predecessor's session is gone (two sweeps apart)")
        elif live_side(rec) == "successor" and now - drain_start(rec, amax) > drain + grace:
            rec = read(target)                     # the DECIDING read, as below
            if not rec or live_side(rec) != "successor":
                continue
            pre = (rec.get("predecessor") or {}).get("tmux") or ""
            why = refuse_kill(rec, pre)
            if why:
                print(f"cc-handoff: {target}'s predecessor outlived its drain and was NOT ended — {why}", file=sys.stderr)
                continue
            tmux("kill-window", "-t", pre)
            finalize(rec, "the predecessor outlived its drain (its --retire never ended it); ended by the sweep")
        elif live_side(rec) == "predecessor" and now > rec.get("deadline", 0) + grace:
            rec = read(target)                     # the DECIDING read: a --ready committing after the loop copied
            if not rec or live_side(rec) != "predecessor" or now <= rec.get("deadline", 0) + grace:
                continue                           # this record means the kill below would hit the LIVE successor
            expire(rec, "the overlap window passed with no --ready")
        elif live_side(rec) == "predecessor" and now - (rec.get("started") or 0) > grace:
            # A SUCCESSOR THAT DIED before --ready: its window closed, or its session exited to a bare shell. Two
            # strikes a sweep apart, as for the predecessor above, because this expiry kills a window.
            if side_live(rec, "successor"):
                if rec.get("succ_gone_since"):
                    rec.pop("succ_gone_since", None)
                    write(rec)
                continue
            gone = rec.get("succ_gone_since") or 0
            if not gone:
                rec["succ_gone_since"] = now
                write(rec)
            elif now - gone >= cfg("CC_HANDOFF_GONE_CONFIRM", 300):
                rec = read(target)                 # the DECIDING read, as above
                if rec and live_side(rec) == "predecessor" and not side_live(rec, "successor"):
                    expire(rec, "the successor's session died before --ready")
    # A `~next` or `~old` window with NO record is one nothing here will ever finish: reported, once per window,
    # not killed — a session with no record is exactly the case where nobody can say whose it is.
    told_p = os.path.join(HANDOFF, "orphans.told")
    try:
        told = json.load(open(told_p))
    except Exception:
        told = {}
    alive = set((tmux("list-windows", "-t", SESSION, "-F", "#{window_id}") or "").split())
    told = {w: t for w, t in told.items() if w in alive}
    for name, wid, secs in orphans():
        if secs < cfg("CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP_MAX", DEFAULT_MAX) or wid in told:
            continue
        told[wid] = now
        line = (f"main:{name} has been up {secs / 3600:.1f} h with no handoff record — nobody will finish it: "
                f"brief it by hand or `tmux kill-window -t {wid}`")
        alert(name, "⚠️ " + line)
        print("cc-handoff: " + line)
    # AND A KIND THAT STOPPED BEING RECOGNISED. Reported the same way and on the same timer: a live session
    # whose window name is not one of the three shapes can never be handed off, and the only reason nobody
    # noticed that of every orchestrator for weeks is that nothing was watching for it.
    for name, wid, secs in unaddressable():
        if secs < cfg("CC_HANDOFF_OVERLAP_MAX", DEFAULT_MAX) or wid in told:
            continue
        told[wid] = now
        line = (f"main:{name} is a live session this handoff cannot address ({SHAPES}) — it can never be "
                f"handed off, and its context shares a key with its neighbours. Teach `parts`/`kind` its shape.")
        alert(name, "⚠️ " + line)
        print("cc-handoff: " + line)
    try:
        os.makedirs(HANDOFF, exist_ok=True)
        with open(told_p + ".tmp", "w") as f:
            json.dump(told, f)
        os.replace(told_p + ".tmp", told_p)
    except OSError:
        pass
    if rotate_too:
        rotate()                                   # ROTATION: last, on the windows the reaping above left as they are
    return 0


def status(target=None, as_json=False):
    recs = [r for r in ([read(target)] if target else read_all()) if r]
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(recs))
        return 0 if recs else 1
    orph = [] if target else orphans()
    if not recs:
        # NAME THE KIND. Asked about one address, the answer says which session that is — the whole point of
        # an address is that `<repo>` and `<repo>@<alias>` are two live sessions in one worktree, and a reader
        # who cannot tell them apart reads one's number for the other's (which is how this row started).
        print("no overlapping handoff is open"
              + (f" for {target} ({ARTICLE[kind(target)]}, window main:{target})" if target else ""))
    for name, wid, secs in orph:
        print(f"{name:<24} orphan    no record — window {wid} up {secs / 3600:.1f} h: brief it by hand, "
              f"or tmux kill-window -t {wid}")
    for name, wid, secs in ([] if target else unaddressable()):
        print(f"{name:<24} UNADDRESSABLE  a live session this handoff cannot take as an address ({SHAPES}) — "
              f"it can never be handed off")
    if not recs:
        return 1
    now = time.time()
    for r in recs:
        left = int((r.get("deadline", 0) - now) / 60)
        alive = "alive" if pane_live(r["successor"]["window"]) else "gone"
        held = r.get("held") or []
        print(f"{r['target']:<24} {r.get('phase', '?'):<9} live={live_side(r):<11} "
              f"{left:>4}m left   successor main:{r['successor']['window']} ({alive})   id {r['id']}"
              + (f"   staying for {len(held)} subagent(s)" if held else ""))
    return 0


# ---- the brief -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS. A handover measured on 2026-09-01 cost ~2M input-equivalent tokens: the successor made 71
# tool calls before `--ready` (6.0M cache reads) and the predecessor answered over 18 turns at a 400k context.
# Almost none of that was new information — it was the same journal, board, queue and PR list read again, one
# tool call at a time, by a session with an empty context. So the box assembles the packet ONCE, in the order
# a successor needs it, and the questions left over are the ones a file genuinely cannot answer.
# THE JOURNAL IS READ FROM THE END. A track journal is a running file; a planning one runs to hundreds of kB.
# The successor needs the last entry and the dated lines after it, and reading the rest is the expense this
# whole command exists to remove — so the file is read backwards, a page at a time, and stops at the entry it
# was after. TWO SHAPES, because the box writes two: a planning journal marks its handoff entries "journal at
# N%" and a track's `progress.md` does not (of the 93 here, none does) — so a missing marker is the NORMAL
# case for a track and must not tip the raw tail into the packet. The marker wins where there is one; where
# there is none the last dated `## ` heading is the entry, and the packet says which of the two it cut on.
# SINCE P3 OF THE LIBRARY this reader is the FALLBACK: the brief carries `cc-lib view` (journal_view, 8 kB, whole
# entries from the last marker, a header naming the ask that reaches the rest) and reads this way only without cc-lib.
BRIEF_CAP = 64 * 1024                # the most of a journal --brief will ever read (the fallback tail; the view is 8 kB)
JOURNAL_MARK = re.compile(r"^## .*journal at \d+%", re.M)
JOURNAL_HEAD = re.compile(r"^## ", re.M)         # any dated entry: what a track journal has instead
ANSI = re.compile(r"\x1b\[[0-9;]*m")
BOARD_ROW = re.compile(r"^  \S+ (\S+) \[([a-z]+)\]")     # "  ▶ <track> [running]  <title>", ANSI stripped
BOARD_CONT = "      "                                     # its indented task/PR/note lines
BOARD_TASK = BOARD_CONT + "task:"                         # the row's brief, truncated: cut, see board_open
BOARD_HELD = BOARD_CONT + "held ("                        # a hold a person put on the row: kept, see board_open
BOARD_FOOT = re.compile(r"^  \(\d+ finished — cc board show ")   # cc-board's own last line, after the rows
DONE_ROWS = ("merged", "done")
BACKLOG_ROWS = ("queued",)         # of cc-board's STATES, the one nobody has started: header only, see board_open
LANE = re.compile(r"^lane[0-9]+$")                        # the tmux land-lane crutch (retired in #154, not gone)
PR_IN = re.compile(r"(?<![0-9])([0-9]{1,6})(?![0-9])")


def out(argv, timeout=25, cwd=None, fail=""):
    """A helper's stdout, or `fail`. A brief never fails: a section the box cannot fill says so and the rest
    is still worth the successor's one call. `fail` is for the sections where EMPTY and BROKEN read the same
    — no open PRs and a `gh` that would not run both print nothing, and only one of them is good news."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout,
                           cwd=cwd if cwd and os.path.isdir(cwd) else None)
        # ...and only where one was asked for: the other sections print whatever a helper managed to say,
        # which is what they did before this argument existed.
        return fail if (fail and r.returncode != 0) else (r.stdout or "")
    except Exception:
        return fail


def last_match(pat, text, pos):
    """The last match of `pat` in `text`, ignoring one at index 0 while bytes remain below it: `^` matches
    there too, and mid-file that is the middle of a line, not the start of one."""
    found = None
    for m in pat.finditer(text):
        if m.start() == 0 and pos > 0:
            continue
        found = m
    return found


def tail_entry(path, cap=BRIEF_CAP):
    """The last journal entry and everything after it, read BACKWARDS from the end of the file. Returns
    (text, bytes_read, anchor) — `anchor` names what it cut on, because the two journal shapes cut on
    different things and the packet says which; `bytes_read` is what the selfcheck pins: never the whole
    file, and never more than `cap`."""
    path = os.path.expanduser(path)
    try:
        size = os.path.getsize(path)
    except OSError:
        return "", 0, "missing file"
    read = 0
    buf = b""
    pos = size
    text = ""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            while pos > 0 and read < cap:
                step = min(8192, pos, cap - read)
                pos -= step
                fh.seek(pos)
                buf = fh.read(step) + buf
                read += step
                text = buf.decode("utf-8", "replace")
                mark = last_match(JOURNAL_MARK, text, pos)
                if mark:
                    return text[mark.start():], read, "last 'journal at N%' entry"
                # NO EARLY FALLBACK. Two whole unmarked "## " entries in the buffer used to be enough to
                # stop here — but a planning journal runs marked entry, then two or three unmarked OVERLAP /
                # FINAL / TAKEOVER entries, so on any page but the last that test fires INSIDE a marked entry
                # and the packet starts below the very entry the successor is told to read (review of #181).
                # A track journal has no marker anywhere, and its fallback is the same one, after the loop.
    except OSError:
        return "", read, "unreadable file"
    head = last_match(JOURNAL_HEAD, text, pos)
    if head:
        return text[head.start():], read, "last dated entry"
    return (f"(no dated '## ' entry in the last {read // 1024} kB of {path} — the tail follows)\n"
            + buf.decode("utf-8", "replace"), read, "raw tail")


VIEW_CAP = 8 * 1024                  # the checkpoint view a brief carries: 8 kB, where the tail could be 64


def journal_view(path):
    """THE CHECKPOINT VIEW of the journal (cc-lib view): the newest whole entries that fit 8 kB, latest last, starting
    at the last `journal at N%` entry where there is one (the marker wins, as tail_entry's rule has it), under a
    header that names how many of how many entries are shown and the `cc-lib ask` that reaches the rest. Returns
    (text, label) — or (None, None) when cc-lib is not beside this file or fails, and the caller falls back to
    tail_entry, so a brief never loses its journal section to the library. subprocess directly, not out(): the
    selfcheck stubs out() and this section needs the real view."""
    lib = os.path.join(BIN, "cc-lib")
    if not os.access(lib, os.X_OK):
        return None, None
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([lib, "view", os.path.expanduser(path), "--cap", str(VIEW_CAP), "--from", r"journal at \d+%"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=15)
    except Exception:
        return None, None
    if r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout.startswith("checkpoint view"):
        return None, None
    head, _, body = r.stdout.partition("\n")
    return body, head[len("checkpoint view: "):]


def board_open(repo):
    """`cc board show <repo>`, minus what a successor cannot act on. Measured on this box's own board
    (2026-09-16): 63 rows printed 32 kB of a 48 kB packet, and the successor re-reads all of it every turn.
    Three cuts, each naming what is left to read it with:
      · a FINISHED row (merged/done) and every line under it — a successor inherits what is unfinished.
      · EVERY row's `task:` line, and the unindented lines its text wraps onto — 12.9 kB of the 32, and it is
        the brief cut off mid-word. `cc board get <repo> <row> instructions` has the whole of it, for the one row picked up.
      · a QUEUED row's notes and delivery lines — 7.1 kB more. A successor acts on what is live; of the backlog
        it need only know the row exists, which the header it keeps says. Every other unfinished state
        (running, waiting, review, blocked) keeps its notes: those are rows something is happening to.
    Two lines are never cut by those rules. A row's `held (<who>): <reason>` line (cc-board: the hold is the
    first thing under the row, and `show` is the only place a person is offered it), because a successor that
    is not told a backlog row is deliberately on hold is the one that starts it. And cc-board's own footer,
    `  (N finished — cc board show <repo> --all)`, which follows the last row and would otherwise read as that
    row's wrapped task text when the task line was the last thing under it.
    """
    keep, done, backlog, wrap = [], False, False, False
    for line in ANSI.sub("", out([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-board"), "show", repo])).splitlines():
        m = BOARD_ROW.match(line)
        if m:
            done, backlog, wrap = m.group(2) in DONE_ROWS, m.group(2) in BACKLOG_ROWS, False
            if line.strip() and not done:
                keep.append(line)
            continue
        if not line.startswith(BOARD_CONT):
            # Unindented: the board's own header (`<repo>  base:main  /path`) or footer, or a line a long
            # `task:` wrapped onto. Only the first two are lines of their own — and before this the wrapped text
            # was read as one, so a FINISHED row's wrapped task text reset the drop and leaked into the packet.
            # The footer is matched by shape, not by `wrap`: after a row whose task line was the last thing
            # under it, `wrap` is still up when the footer arrives (review of #521).
            if wrap and not BOARD_FOOT.match(line):
                continue
            done = backlog = wrap = False
            if line.strip():
                keep.append(line)
            continue
        wrap = line.startswith(BOARD_TASK)
        # "keep a line starting with BOARD_CONT + 'held (' regardless of backlog (only done drops it)" — #521
        if done or wrap or (backlog and not line.startswith(BOARD_HELD)):
            continue
        keep.append(line)
    return "\n".join(keep)


def lanes():
    """What is LANDING right now. Two shapes, because the box has both: a `lane<N>` tmux window (the crutch
    #154 retired — one left running is exactly the thing a successor must be told about) and the queue that
    replaced it, one job file per PR under ~/.cc/state/land/."""
    rows = []
    for line in (tmux("list-windows", "-a", "-F",
                      "#{window_name}\t#{window_id}\t#{pane_pid}\t#{pane_start_command}") or "").splitlines():
        fields = line.split("\t")
        if len(fields) == 4 and LANE.match(fields[0]):
            pr = PR_IN.search(fields[3].replace(fields[0], " "))
            rows.append(f"{fields[0]:<10} window {fields[1]:<6} PR {pr.group(1) if pr else '?':<6} waiter pid {fields[2]}")
    land = os.path.join(STATE, "land")
    try:
        jobs = sorted(f for f in os.listdir(land) if f.endswith(".json"))
    except OSError:
        jobs = []
    for j in jobs:
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(land, j)) as fh:
                r = json.load(fh)
        except Exception:
            continue
        rows.append(f"queue      {r.get('repo', '?')} PR {r.get('pr', '?')}   stage {r.get('stage', '?')}   "
                    f"attempt {r.get('attempts', 0)}   queued {r.get('queued_at', '?')}")
    return "\n".join(rows)


def live_workers(repo):
    """One line per track whose loop is still running: the last line of its loop.log, which is where a worker
    says where it got to."""
    rows = []
    base = os.path.join(STATE, repo)
    try:
        tracks = sorted(os.listdir(base))
    except OSError:
        return ""
    idle = cfg("CC_BRIEF_WORKER_IDLE", 3600)              # once, not once per track: a repo has scores of them
    for t in tracks:
        log = os.path.join(base, t, "loop.log")
        try:
            if time.time() - os.path.getmtime(log) > idle:
                continue                                  # long finished: the board carries those
            last = ""
            for last in open(log, errors="replace"):
                pass
        except OSError:
            continue
        rows.append(f"{t:<24} {last.strip()[:160]}")
    return "\n".join(rows)


def prs(repo):
    """The TARGET's open PRs. `gh` resolves a repo from its cwd, and --brief is run from wherever the caller
    happens to be: from another repo's checkout it printed that repo's PRs under this one's heading, and from
    a dir that is no checkout at all it printed none — which a successor reads as "nothing is open". So it is
    asked inside the target's own checkout, and a gh that could not answer says so rather than showing empty."""
    where = os.path.join(DEV, repo)          # DEV is already absolute; the selfcheck points it at its own
    if not os.path.isdir(os.path.join(where, ".git")):
        return f"(unavailable: {where} is not a checkout, so gh has no repo to list)"
    return out(["gh", "pr", "list", "--limit", "40", "--json", "number,headRefName,mergeable", "--template",
                "{{range .}}{{printf \"%-6v %-38v %v\\n\" .number .headRefName .mergeable}}{{end}}"],
               cwd=where, fail=f"(unavailable: gh could not list the open PRs of {repo})")


def brief(target):
    """--brief: ONE packet, in the order a successor needs it, so it does not rebuild it a tool call at a
    time. Read-only and best-effort throughout — this must never be the thing that fails a handoff."""
    repo = parts(target)[0]
    rec = read(target)
    journal, jlabel = journal_view(journal_for(target))
    if journal is None:   # no cc-lib: the 64 kB tail it was
        journal, jread, janchor = tail_entry(journal_for(target))
        jlabel = f"the {janchor} and every line after it ({jread // 1024 or 1} kB read of {journal_for(target)}, never the whole file)"
    pre = (rec or {}).get("predecessor") or {}
    who = [f"target        {target}",
           f"predecessor   window main:{pre.get('window') or target}   tmux {pre.get('tmux') or win_id(target) or '?'}",
           f"              session {(pre.get('session_id') or '?')}   (SendMessage/ListAgents match on the tmux id)"]
    if rec:
        who.append(f"handoff       id {rec.get('id')}   phase {rec.get('phase')}   live {live_side(rec)}   "
                   f"{int((rec.get('deadline', 0) - time.time()) / 60)}m left")
    sections = [
        (f"journal — checkpoint view: {jlabel}", journal),
        # No `task:` line and no notes on a queued row: `cc board get <repo> <row> instructions` has both, for the one
        # row the successor actually picks up. board_open says why.
        (f"board — {repo}, unfinished rows, without their task text; a queued row is its header and any hold on it. "
         f"`cc board get {repo} <row> instructions` has the whole of any one of them", board_open(repo)),
        ("land — lanes and the landing queue", lanes()),
        (f"workers — live loops under {repo}, last line of each", live_workers(repo)),
        # the predecessor's own work still out: what --ready will wait for, and what it will leave running
        ("still out — the predecessor's subagents and the workers it dispatched", out_line(target, rec)),
        ("open PRs", prs(repo)),
        # `list`, not `count`: a successor who inherits "49 open asks" as a bare number cannot see which ones,
        # and asks for them. `list` with no --status is the same set `count` counted — the unsettled ones, one
        # line each with the ask text cut at 96 chars, and a second line on the few that are merged or declined.
        # This box's own 49 print as 5 kB, against the 64 kB of BRIEF_CAP the journal alone may spend.
        ("scope — open asks from the owner", out([os.path.join(BIN, "cc-scope"), "list", repo])),
        ("who", "\n".join(who)),
    ]
    lines = [f"cc-handoff --brief {target}  ({time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', time.gmtime())})"]
    for title, body in sections:
        lines += [f"\n== {title} ==", body.rstrip() if body.strip() else "(none)"]
    lines.append(f"\nThat is the whole packet. What is left is what no file holds: ask your predecessor ONE batch of "
                 f"questions about it, and run `cc-handoff --ready` when they are answered.")
    packet = "\n".join(lines) + "\n"
    sys.stdout.write(packet)
    sys.stdout.flush()                                 # the successor has its packet before the ledger is asked
    record_packet(target, rec, len(packet.encode()))
    return 0


PACKET_KIND = "spend/handoff-packet-over-band"
PACKET_BAND = 24 * 1024            # bytes; CC_BRIEF_PACKET_BAND overrides it


def record_packet(target, rec, size):
    """A packet at or over the band is one failures record, once per handoff (the record's id; with no handoff open,
    once per target per UTC day): the successor re-reads all of it every turn. Best-effort like the rest of --brief —
    the packet is already out, so a writer that fails is one stderr line."""
    band = int(cfg("CC_BRIEF_PACKET_BAND", PACKET_BAND))
    if size < band:                                    # "crosses a band": at the band is over it
        return
    key = (rec or {}).get("id") or time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d", time.gmtime())
    cmd = [os.path.join(BIN, "cc-failures"), "record", "--once", "--scope", parts(target)[0], "--kind", PACKET_KIND,
           "--writer", "cc-handoff", "--detector", "box", "--detector-evidence", "cc-handoff --brief measured the packet it printed",
           "--observed", f"the handoff packet for {target} was {size:,} bytes, over the {band:,}-byte band: "
                         f"the successor re-reads all of it every turn",
           "--expected", f"a handoff packet under {band:,} bytes", "--cost", f"packet_bytes={size}",
           "--link", f"source=brief:{target}:{key}", "--link", f"target={target}"]
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=10)
        if r.returncode != 0:
            print(f"cc-handoff: packet over the band, not recorded: "
                  f"{((r.stderr or r.stdout).strip().splitlines() or ['cc-failures record failed'])[-1]}", file=sys.stderr)
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"cc-handoff: packet over the band, not recorded: {e.__class__.__name__}", file=sys.stderr)


# ---- selfcheck -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def selfcheck():
    """The cases are core/tests/handoff_selfcheck.py, run in THIS namespace so their `global` rebinds and stubs land on
    the tool as they did when the body was here. Exit 1 on any failure."""
    path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(BIN), "tests", "handoff_selfcheck.py")
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        exec(compile(fh.read(), path, "exec"), globals())
    return globals()["run_selfcheck"]()


# ---- cli -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def main(argv):
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
        print(__doc__.strip())
        return 0
    if argv[0] == "selfcheck":
        return selfcheck()
    cmd, rest = argv[0], argv[1:]
    opt = {}
    pos = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(rest):
        a = rest[i]
        if a in ("--session", "--why", "--in-flight"):
            opt[a[2:].replace("-", "_")] = rest[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(rest) else ""
            i += 1
        elif a in ("--forced", "--json", "--rotate"):
            opt[a.lstrip("-")] = True
        elif a.startswith("--"):
            print(f"cc-handoff: unknown option {a}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        else:
            pos.append(a)
        i += 1
    target = pos[0] if pos else None
    if target and not TARGET_RE.match(target):
        print(f"cc-handoff: '{target}' is not a target ({SHAPES})", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if cmd == "--overlap":
        if not target:
            print("cc-handoff: --overlap needs a target", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        return start(target, opt.get("session", ""), opt.get("in_flight", ""))
    if cmd == "--replace":
        if not target:
            print("cc-handoff: --replace needs a member workspace", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        return replace(target, opt.get("session", ""))
    if cmd == "--kick":                            # internal: the detached child start() leaves behind
        return kick(target, pos[1] if len(pos) > 1 else "") if target else 1
    if cmd == "--ready":
        return ready(target, bool(opt.get("forced")))
    if cmd == "--sweep":
        return sweep(bool(opt.get("rotate")))
    if cmd == "--abandon":
        return abandon(target, opt.get("why", "")) if target else 1
    if cmd == "--retire":
        return retire(target) if target else 1
    if cmd == "--brief":
        if not target:
            print(f"cc-handoff: --brief needs a target ({SHAPES})", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        return brief(target)
    if cmd == "--status":
        return status(target, bool(opt.get("json")))
    print(__doc__.strip(), file=sys.stderr)
    return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
